/* Question bank PA test (English convenience translation), Wft Non-life Insurance (Personal). Vorm: [vraag, [[antwoord, uitleg] x4], toetsterm CDFD]; het EERSTE antwoord is steeds het juiste.
   Gegenereerd met tools/vragen-js.py. */
(window.PA_VRAGEN = window.PA_VRAGEN || {})["schade-particulier"] = [
["At what moment does the statutory duty of disclosure of the policyholder apply under Book 7, Title 17 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW)?", [["Before the insurance contract is concluded.", "The statutory duty of disclosure relates to the pre-contractual phase; changes during the term only have to be reported if the policy says so."], ["Throughout the entire term of the insurance.", "The law does not provide for a continuing duty of disclosure; a duty to report a change in risk follows only from the policy conditions."], ["Only at the moment a claim is reported.", "When damage occurs there is a duty to report and provide information, but that is a different obligation from the pre-contractual duty of disclosure."], ["Only at every tacit renewal of the policy.", "A renewal is not a new conclusion of the contract in which the statutory duty of disclosure arises again."]], "1a.8"],
["Which facts must a policyholder report under the duty of disclosure?", [["Facts that he knows or ought to know and that are relevant to the insurer's decision.", "That is the statutory standard: it concerns the policyholder's knowledge and the relevance to the acceptance and premium decision."], ["Only facts that the policyholder himself considers important for the level of the premium.", "The standard is not the customer's subjective judgement, but what he ought to understand about the insurer's decision."], ["All facts about his person, family and finances, even if they play no role in the risk or the premium.", "The duty is limited to facts that may be important for the insurer's decision."], ["Only facts that appear from public registers, such as the Land Registry or the trade register.", "Being public is not decisive; facts that are not registered, such as earlier claims, may also have to be reported."]], "1a.8"],
["An insurer uses a questionnaire at the time of application. After a claim, a relevant fact turns out to exist that was not asked about. What applies?", [["The insurer cannot rely on it, unless the customer intended to mislead the insurer.", "With a questionnaire the customer may rely on the questions asked; only where there is intent to mislead the insurer is this different."], ["The insurer may reduce the payment proportionally, because the fact was relevant.", "Proportional reduction is a sanction for breach of the duty of disclosure, but in principle the insurer cannot rely on facts that were not asked about."], ["The insurer may annul the policy retroactively because of mistake about the risk.", "For facts that were not asked about, there is no ground for such a claim without intent to mislead."], ["The insurer does not have to pay, because the customer also had to report everything unprompted.", "Precisely where a questionnaire is used, the law in practice limits the duty of disclosure to the questions asked."]], "1a.8"],
["Mrs Bakker (52) takes out a home contents insurance through her adviser. The application form asks about claims in the past five years. She forgets to report a burglary from two years ago, without any intention of misleading the insurer. After a new burglary the insurer discovers this and shows that, had it been reported correctly, it would have accepted her at a higher premium. What is in principle the consequence?", [["The insurer pays in the proportion of the premium paid to the premium that would have applied had it been reported correctly.", "This is the statutory proportionality rule where there was no intent to mislead and the insurer would only have charged a higher premium."], ["The insurer may refuse payment entirely, because she did not report a fact that was expressly asked about.", "Without intent, complete refusal is only possible if the insurer would not have concluded any insurance at all had it been reported correctly."], ["The insurer pays in full, but may increase the premium for the future.", "Where a breach affected the premium, the payment is in fact reduced proportionally; payment in full does not fit with that."], ["The insurer may still set off the old burglary claim against the new payment.", "The law has no such set-off; the consequences depend on what the insurer would have done had it been reported correctly."]], "1a.8"],
["Within what period must an insurer, after discovering a breach of the duty of disclosure, point this out to the policyholder in order to be able to rely on the consequences?", [["Within two months of the discovery.", "The insurer must point out the non-compliance and state the consequences within two months of discovery; otherwise its right to rely on it lapses."], ["Within fourteen days of the discovery.", "Fourteen days is the reminder period for premium payment, not the period for the duty of disclosure."], ["Within three years of the discovery.", "Three years is the limitation period for claims against the insurer; a much shorter period applies to this reliance."], ["No period applies; the insurer can always rely on it.", "The law in fact sets a period to give the policyholder certainty."]], "1a.8"],
["What may an insurer do if the policyholder breached the duty of disclosure with the intent to mislead the insurer?", [["Refuse payment and terminate the insurance with immediate effect.", "In the case of intent to mislead, the right to payment lapses and the insurer may terminate the contract immediately."], ["Only increase the premium retroactively.", "In the case of intent to mislead, the law goes further than premium adjustment; the customer loses his right to payment."], ["Only pay out proportionally.", "Proportional payment is the main rule without intent; in the case of intent to mislead the insurer does not have to pay anything."], ["Terminate the insurance only at the next contract expiry date, while preserving the right to payment.", "In the case of intent to mislead, the law permits termination with immediate effect and the right to payment lapses."]], "1a.8"],
["The policyholder does not pay a renewal premium on time. When may the insurer suspend cover?", [["After an unsuccessful reminder to pay within fourteen days.", "For renewal premiums, default only arises after a reminder with a payment period of fourteen days; after that, cover may be suspended."], ["Immediately on the day after the due date, without a prior reminder.", "For renewal premiums a reminder is required first; suspension without a reminder is not permitted."], ["Only after the premium arrears have lasted three months.", "The law has no three-month period; the decisive factor is the unsuccessful reminder with a period of fourteen days."], ["Only after the court has permitted the suspension of cover.", "Court intervention is not needed; suspension follows from the policy once default has occurred."]], "1a.4"],
["What must an insured do under the law as soon as he knows or ought to know that there is damage for which cover may exist?", [["Report the damage as soon as reasonably possible and provide the necessary information.", "This is the statutory duty to report and to provide information after a loss event."], ["First have the damage repaired himself and then send in the repair invoice with photos.", "Repairing it on his own authority may make assessment of the damage more difficult; the law first requires timely reporting and information."], ["Report the damage only once it is established which party is liable.", "The duty to report does not depend on the question of liability; reporting must take place as soon as possible."], ["Report the damage within a year, because otherwise every right lapses.", "The law states no fixed reporting period of one year; the standard is 'as soon as reasonably possible'."]], "1h.4"],
["While Frank de Wit is on holiday, a tap in the bathroom starts to leak. After returning home he sees the water damage, but because of being busy he reports it to his insurer only four months later. Meanwhile mould has developed and the cause is harder to establish. Frank did not intend to mislead the insurer. What may the insurer in principle do?", [["Reduce the payment by the disadvantage it suffered because of the late report.", "Without intent to mislead, the insurer may only deduct the disadvantage of the late report, such as the additional mould damage."], ["Refuse payment entirely, because Frank greatly exceeded the reporting period in the policy.", "Complete forfeiture of the right to payment in the case of a late report is only possible where there is intent to mislead."], ["Pay in full, because reporting late without intent has no consequences at all.", "Reporting late does have consequences: the insurer may deduct the disadvantage it suffers as a result."], ["Terminate the insurance retroactively from the date of the damage.", "Termination with retroactive effect is not a consequence that the law attaches to a late damage report."]], "1h.14"],
["What compensation applies under Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW) for reasonable costs incurred by the insured to prevent or limit imminent damage (mitigation costs)?", [["They are reimbursed, even if the sum insured is exceeded as a result.", "Mitigation costs are for the account of the insurer, even above the sum insured, because mitigation also serves the insurer."], ["They are reimbursed only if the measure actually limited the damage.", "Reasonable measures without result are also reimbursed; success is not a condition."], ["They are not reimbursed, because they fall under the insured's excess.", "The law in fact obliges the insurer to reimburse reasonable mitigation costs."], ["They are reimbursed up to a maximum of half the sum insured under the policy.", "The law has no maximum of half the sum insured; reimbursement may even exceed the sum insured."]], "1h.15"],
["What does the law say about damage that an insured causes intentionally or through recklessness under a non-life insurance?", [["The insurer pays no compensation to that insured.", "The law excludes payment to the insured who caused the damage intentionally or through recklessness."], ["The insurer pays half.", "In the case of intent or recklessness, the law has no partial payment to that insured."], ["The insurer pays in full, but may terminate the policy afterwards.", "Payment is in fact excluded in the case of intent or recklessness by that insured."], ["Only intent is excluded by law; recklessness is always covered.", "The statutory provision names recklessness in addition to intent."]], "1a.5"],
["What is the main rule under Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW) for damage caused by an inherent defect of the insured item?", [["That damage is not reimbursed, unless otherwise agreed.", "Inherent defect is a statutory exclusion of non-mandatory law; the policy may provide cover."], ["That damage is always reimbursed, because the item is insured.", "The law in fact excludes damage caused by inherent defect, unless the policy provides otherwise."], ["That damage is reimbursed only under all-risks cover, by virtue of the law.", "It is the policy, not the law, that may insure inherent defect as well."], ["That damage falls under mitigation costs.", "Mitigation costs are costs to limit damage; inherent defect is a cause of damage."]], "1a.5"],
["Fatma and Erik Groen have insured their home for €200,000. A few years ago they had a large extension built, but never notified the insurer. As a result the rebuilding value is now €250,000. There is no guarantee against underinsurance and no first-loss cover. After a fire the damage amounts to €50,000. How much does the insurer pay?", [["€40,000.", "There is underinsurance. Under the proportionality rule, 200,000/250,000 × €50,000 = €40,000 is reimbursed."], ["€50,000.", "Full reimbursement applies only without underinsurance or with first-loss insurance; here the home is insured too low."], ["€37,500.", "This is not an outcome of the proportionality rule; the ratio between the insured amount and the value is 0.8, so €40,000."], ["€25,000.", "The proportionality rule does not halve the damage; the reimbursement is 80% of €50,000."]], "1h.7"],
["Someone has covered the same risk with two insurers (concurrence). What is the starting point under Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW)?", [["He has his damage reimbursed once; the insurers divide it among themselves.", "Concurrence does not break the indemnity principle: the insured has his damage reimbursed once and the insurers have mutual recourse."], ["He receives the full damage from both insurers, because he pays two premiums.", "Double reimbursement is contrary to the indemnity principle."], ["Only the insurer with the oldest policy pays; the younger policy lapses.", "The age of the policy is not the statutory standard for concurrence."], ["Both insurances are void, because double cover is prohibited by law.", "Concurrence does not make insurances void; the law in fact regulates how they pay together."]], "1d.5"],
["What does subrogation entail in a non-life insurance?", [["After payment, the right of recourse against the liable third party passes to the insurer.", "Through subrogation the insurer can recover the damage paid out from the party who caused it."], ["The insured may have both the insurer and the liable third party pay in full.", "The insured would then receive double; subrogation in fact prevents that."], ["After a payment the insurer may unilaterally increase the insured's premium.", "Premium adjustment has nothing to do with subrogation."], ["When switching, a new insurer takes over the current policy with all rights.", "Switching is a new contract; subrogation concerns the transfer of rights of recourse."]], "1h.13"],
["What is the limitation period for a legal claim for payment against an insurer?", [["Three years after the day following the day on which the claimant became aware that the payment is due.", "That is the statutory period; interruption is possible by a written communication."], ["Five years after the day on which the loss event took place or was discovered.", "Five years is the relative limitation period for a wrongful act, not the period for claims against an insurer."], ["Twenty years after the day on which the insurance contract was concluded.", "Twenty years is an absolute period from liability law, not the period for claims against an insurer."], ["One year after the day on which the damage was reported to the insurer.", "The law has no period of one year for this claim."]], "1h.13"],
["The insurer rejects a claim unambiguously in writing and points out the consequences. What happens to the limitation period?", [["A new limitation period of six months begins.", "After an unambiguous acknowledgement or rejection, a new period of six months runs."], ["The limitation period of three years begins to run again.", "After a clear rejection the new period is in fact shorter: six months."], ["The claim is time-barred immediately from the day of rejection.", "A rejection does not make the claim time-barred immediately; the customer still has six months to take action."], ["The limitation has been interrupted for good and can no longer occur.", "Interruption leads to a new period, not to a claim that can never become time-barred."]], "1h.13"],
["A consumer has a home contents insurance that was tacitly renewed after the first contract year. What applies to cancellation by the consumer?", [["He can cancel at any time, notice period at most one month.", "After tacit renewal a consumer may cancel daily with a notice period of at most one month."], ["He can cancel only at the next annual contract expiry date.", "That no longer applies after tacit renewal; the consumer may cancel at any time."], ["He can cancel, but must observe a notice period of three months.", "A notice period of more than one month may not be stipulated after tacit renewal."], ["He can cancel only after the insurer has increased the premium.", "The law does not limit the right of cancellation to a premium increase."]], "1a.9"],
["What is the essential difference between a non-life insurance and a fixed-sum insurance?", [["A non-life insurance compensates financial loss; a fixed-sum insurance pays a fixed amount.", "This is the statutory distinction; the indemnity principle applies only to non-life insurance."], ["A non-life insurance is always compulsory by law; a fixed-sum insurance is always a voluntary choice.", "Being compulsory is independent of the type: most non-life insurances are voluntary."], ["A fixed-sum insurance always has an excess; a non-life insurance never does.", "An excess occurs mainly in non-life insurance; it is not a distinguishing feature."], ["A non-life insurance pays only on death; a fixed-sum insurance pays on damage.", "Payment on death is typical of a fixed-sum insurance, such as a life insurance."]], "1a.2"],
["A collector has his painting appraised by an expert before taking out the insurance, and the insurer accepts the appraisal. What is the consequence of this in the event of damage?", [["The appraised value applies in principle, unless the insurer proves fraud.", "An appraisal determined in advance by an expert prevents discussion about the value; the insurer can in principle only challenge it in the case of fraud."], ["The insurer may always have the value determined again after damage.", "That is precisely why an appraisal is made in advance: the insurer is in principle bound by the appraisal."], ["In the case of total loss the insurer pays twice the appraised value.", "An appraisal fixes the value; the indemnity principle does not allow double payment."], ["The appraisal is relevant only for the level of the premium, not for the damage.", "The appraisal is relevant mainly for the damage payment."]], "1h.17"],
["A consumer takes out a travel insurance online for a two-week trip. Can he cancel the contract free of charge within fourteen days under the rules on distance selling of financial services?", [["No, short-term travel and baggage insurances (shorter than one month) are excluded.", "Short-term travel and baggage insurances are excluded from the cooling-off period for distance selling of financial services."], ["Yes, for every insurance concluded at a distance there is a fourteen-day cooling-off period.", "Exceptions apply, including short-term travel and baggage insurances."], ["Yes, but only as long as the trip has not yet started and no claim has been reported.", "The law makes no such exception; for these insurances there is no right of withdrawal."], ["No, a right of withdrawal at a distance exists for no non-life insurance whatsoever.", "For many non-life insurances concluded at a distance, a cooling-off period of fourteen days does apply."]], "1a.9"],
["What is the 'insurable interest' in a non-life insurance?", [["The financial interest of the insured in the absence of damage.", "Without an interest the insured suffers no damage and a non-life insurance serves no purpose."], ["The amount of the annual premium that the policyholder pays.", "The premium is the price of the cover, not the interest."], ["The profit that the insurer is expected to make on the policy.", "The insurable interest is that of the insured, not that of the insurer."], ["The part of the damage that the insured bears himself per event as excess.", "The excess is the part of the damage borne by the insured himself, not the insurable interest."]], "1c.3"],
["What does the indemnity principle entail?", [["The insured may not be financially better off as a result of the payment.", "A non-life insurance compensates the damage, no more; this prevents enrichment and an incentive to cause damage."], ["The insurer must pay the full sum insured for every claim.", "The sum insured is a maximum; the actual damage is paid."], ["The insured is, by law, always entitled to replacement value.", "Replacement value is a permitted but contractual deviation; the principle itself is about preventing enrichment."], ["The insured may claim the same damage from several insurers.", "Double reimbursement is in fact contrary to the indemnity principle."]], "1a.7"],
["Why is reimbursement on a replacement-value basis accepted for home contents, despite the indemnity principle?", [["Because otherwise the insured must pay extra for replacement; policy limits restrict enrichment.", "Replacement value is an accepted deviation; with limits, such as current value for heavily aged items, enrichment remains limited."], ["Because under the law the indemnity principle does not apply to home contents insurances.", "The principle applies to all non-life insurances; replacement value is a contractual deviation."], ["Because the law prescribes replacement-value reimbursement for all personal non-life insurances.", "The law does not prescribe replacement value; it is an agreement in the policy."], ["Because in practice replacement value always turns out lower than the current value of an item.", "It is the other way round: replacement value is equal to or higher than current value."]], "1a.7"],
["What is the difference between a deductible franchise and an integral franchise?", [["Deductible: the excess is always subtracted from the damage; integral: above the threshold everything is reimbursed.", "With an integral franchise the threshold is a limit: damage below it is not reimbursed, damage above it is reimbursed in full."], ["Integral: the insured always pays the whole damage; deductible: only small claims.", "Above the threshold, an insurer with an integral franchise in fact reimburses everything."], ["Deductible: applies only to liability; integral: applies only to fire and storm insurances.", "Deductible franchises occur in almost all non-life insurances."], ["There is no difference; both terms describe the same fixed excess per claim.", "The way they work clearly differs for claims above the threshold."]], "1d.3"],
["Mr De Vries has a comprehensive (all-risks) insurance for his two-year-old car, with an excess of €250 per event. While reversing he hits a concrete post. The damage to his car amounts to €3,000 and is covered. There is no counterparty from whom to recover the damage. What does the insurer pay?", [["€2,750.", "The excess works as a deductible franchise: €3,000 − €250 = €2,750."], ["€3,000.", "That would only be correct for an integral franchise that is exceeded; an excess is always subtracted from the damage."], ["€2,500.", "Here the excess has been deducted twice; it applies once per event."], ["€0.", "No payment would apply only if the damage is lower than the excess."]], "1d.3"],
["Wouter and Esther have three young children and drive the family car a lot, including with the children's friends. Wouter is self-employed (zzp'er) without disability insurance. In the event of an accident they want the actual damage of themselves and their passengers to be reimbursed, such as loss of income and recovery costs. That must also apply if Wouter causes the accident himself. Their car insurance currently has only third-party liability (WA) and own-damage cover (casco). Which additional cover best fits this wish?", [["A passenger damage insurance (SVI).", "The SVI reimburses the actual personal injury and property damage of all occupants, including the driver and regardless of who is at fault."], ["A passenger accident insurance (OVI).", "An OVI pays fixed amounts on death or permanent disability and does not reimburse actual loss of income."], ["A higher insured amount on the WA cover.", "Higher WA cover only helps with liability towards others, not with the driver's own injury."], ["Legal expenses cover for recovery of damages with the car insurance.", "Legal expenses cover for recovery helps with recourse against a liable counterparty, but reimburses nothing if Wouter causes the accident himself."]], "1d.9"],
["What characterises all-risks cover for home contents or a dwelling compared with cover based on named perils?", [["Sudden, unforeseen damage is covered, except what is excluded.", "With all-risks there is in principle cover unless an exclusion applies; with named perils the cause must appear in the list."], ["All conceivable damage is covered, without any exclusion in the policy.", "All-risks also has exclusions, such as wear and tear, inherent defect or intent."], ["Only damage caused by fire, storm and burglary is covered, and nothing else.", "That is a limited cover based on named perils."], ["Damage is covered without an excess, because all-risks is the most expensive form.", "All-risks covers in fact often have an excess, certainly for 'other' damage."]], "1c.5"],
["What does an insurance 'on first-loss basis' entail?", [["Reimbursement up to the sum insured, without proportionality in the case of underinsurance.", "With first-loss insurance, underinsurance is not applied; the sum is the maximum."], ["Reimbursement of only the first claim per insurance year, and nothing afterwards.", "First loss is not about the number of claims, but about the absence of proportionality."], ["The insured bears the first part of every claim himself as excess.", "That is an excess, not first-loss cover."], ["The cover only starts after a waiting period from the policy's start date.", "A waiting period has nothing to do with first loss."]], "1d.6"],
["Which damage is usually not covered by the insurance even under all-risks cover?", [["Gradual wear and tear and ageing of an item.", "Wear and tear is not sudden and unforeseen and is therefore excluded as standard."], ["A television that suddenly falls off the cabinet and breaks.", "This is a sudden and unforeseen event, the typical example of all-risks damage."], ["Ink accidentally spilled on a sofa.", "An unexpected accident in the home is as a rule covered under all-risks, possibly with an excess."], ["Fire damage to the kitchen.", "Fire is covered even under more limited covers, so certainly under all-risks."]], "1h.2"],
["On whom does the compulsory insurance duty rest under the Motor Vehicles Liability Insurance Act (WAM)?", [["On the registered keeper of the motor vehicle.", "The WAM places the duty on the keeper; the Netherlands Vehicle Authority (RDW) checks whether a WA insurance exists for every registration number."], ["On each driver individually.", "The insurance does cover the liability of each driver, but the duty to insure lies with the keeper."], ["On the manufacturer or dealer of the vehicle.", "Manufacturers and dealers have no WAM obligation for customers' cars."], ["On the owner of the land on which the car is parked.", "The duty to insure attaches to the vehicle and the keeper, not to the parking place."]], "1f.2"],
["What special right does the WAM give to an injured party?", [["A right of their own to claim directly against the third-party liability (WA) insurer of the party who caused the damage.", "Thanks to this direct action, the injured party does not have to take proceedings via the party who caused the damage."], ["The right to have the premium of the party who caused the damage increased.", "The injured party has no say over someone else's premium."], ["The right to reimbursement of all damage by his own own-damage (casco) insurer, regardless of fault.", "The WAM regulates the position of the injured party towards the WA insurer, not towards his own casco."], ["The right to have the driving licence of the party who caused the damage withdrawn.", "Driving licence measures are matters of criminal or administrative law, not regulated in the WAM."]], "1f.2"],
["After a birthday party, Kees drives home with too much alcohol in his blood. At a junction he hits Ingrid's car. Ingrid has damage to her car and minor injuries. The conditions of Kees' car insurance exclude damage when driving under the influence. What applies to Ingrid's damage?", [["Kees' WA insurer compensates Ingrid and then recovers the amount from Kees.", "Under the WAM the insurer cannot invoke the exclusion against the injured party; it does have recourse against its own insured."], ["Ingrid gets nothing from the insurer and must recover her damage from Kees herself.", "This ignores the injured party's own right; the exclusion works only between the insurer and Kees."], ["The Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer) compensates the damage, because the policy does not cover it.", "The Guarantee Fund comes into play for an unknown or uninsured motor vehicle; Kees' car is insured."], ["Ingrid must first claim from her own casco insurer, which then claims from Kees.", "Ingrid may claim directly from Kees' WA insurer; her own casco insurance is not needed for that."]], "1f.2"],
["When can an injured party in traffic call on the Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer)?", [["When the damage was caused by an unknown or uninsured motor vehicle.", "The Guarantee Fund absorbs damage that would otherwise remain uncompensated, for example in a hit-and-run after an accident."], ["Always, if his own casco insurance does not cover the damage to the car.", "The fund is not a safety net for missing casco cover of one's own."], ["When the injured party caused the collision himself and has no casco.", "Whoever causes the damage himself has no claim on the fund for his own damage."], ["Only for damage caused abroad by a Dutch person.", "The fund is intended for damage in the Netherlands caused by unknown or uninsured motor vehicles."]], "1h.22"],
["Joris is driving his car through a residential area. Suddenly 10-year-old Mila cycles into the road between two parked cars. Joris cannot avoid her and Mila breaks her arm. Mila did not act intentionally or with conscious recklessness and there is no question of force majeure on Joris' side. What applies to her personal injury damage?", [["Joris' WA insurer in principle compensates her full damage.", "For children under 14 the 100% rule applies: their own fault is not held against them, unless there is intent or conscious recklessness."], ["Mila receives at least half, because she herself made a traffic error.", "The 50% rule applies to non-motorised road users aged 14 and over; for younger children 100% applies."], ["Mila receives nothing, because the accident arose from her own fault.", "A child under 14's own fault is in fact not held against them under Article 185 of the Road Traffic Act (WVW)."], ["Mila's parents must bear the damage through their own private liability insurance (AVP).", "The parents' AVP covers their liability towards others, not the injury of their own child."]], "1f.4"],
["Gert (45) cycles through a red light at a junction. He is hit by a car that is driving slightly too fast and breaks his wrist. He cannot work for six weeks. The motorist cannot rely on force majeure. What applies to Gert's personal injury damage?", [["He receives at least 50%; with less contributory fault, more.", "For non-motorised road users aged 14 and over the 50% rule applies: contributory fault reduces the compensation, but he receives at least half, unless there is intent or conscious recklessness."], ["He receives nothing, because he himself rode through a red light.", "Because of the 50% rule, an adult cyclist in principle receives at least half of his personal injury damage even in the case of his own traffic error."], ["He receives everything, because a cyclist is always compensated in full.", "Full compensation applies to children under 14; for adults contributory fault counts above 50%."], ["He receives something only if he has his own accident insurance.", "His claim against the motorist and the motorist's WA insurer is independent of any accident insurance of his own."]], "1h.23"],
["Sandra van Leeuwen is driving home alone in the evening. On a slippery road surface she loses control through her own fault and hits a tree. She suffers a broken hip and cannot work for months. For her car she has only a WA insurance, without additional cover. Which insurance would have compensated her actual personal injury damage, such as loss of income and costs?", [["A passenger damage insurance (SVI).", "The SVI reimburses the actual personal injury and property damage of all occupants, including the driver who causes the accident himself."], ["The WA insurance, after deduction of the excess.", "The WA insurance covers only liability towards others, not the driver's own injury."], ["The Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer).", "The Guarantee Fund compensates victims of unknown or uninsured motor vehicles, not a driver who causes the accident himself."], ["A passenger accident insurance (OVI).", "An OVI pays only fixed amounts, such as on death or permanent disability; it does not reimburse actual damage such as loss of income."]], "1d.9"],
["What is the difference between a passenger damage insurance (SVI) and a passenger accident insurance (OVI)?", [["The SVI compensates the actual damage; the OVI pays fixed amounts.", "The SVI is a non-life insurance, the OVI a fixed-sum insurance; as a result the SVI usually offers broader compensation."], ["The SVI applies only to the driver; the OVI only to the passengers.", "Both insurances in principle apply to all occupants, including the driver."], ["The OVI also compensates damage to the car; the SVI covers only personal injury.", "Neither is a casco insurance; the SVI does compensate damage to occupants' belongings."], ["There is no difference; they are two names for the same insurance product.", "The difference between actual damage and fixed amounts is essential."]], "1d.9"],
["Which damage is usually NOT covered under a limited own-damage (partial casco) insurance?", [["Damage to one's own car from a collision caused by one's own fault.", "Damage caused by one's own fault is the distinguishing difference between limited and full casco."], ["Theft of the car from a public car park.", "Theft is a core peril of limited casco."], ["Windscreen damage from a stone thrown up on the motorway.", "Windscreen damage usually falls under limited casco."], ["Damage from a tree falling on the parked car during a storm.", "Storm and other natural forces usually fall under limited casco."]], "1c.7"],
["Mr Jansen has a four-year-old car, insured with WA and limited casco. While parking in a car park he drives into a concrete pillar. The bumper and a door are damaged and there is no counterparty. He asks his adviser whether his insurance covers the damage to his car. What is the correct answer?", [["No, damage from a collision of one's own making is covered only under a full casco insurance.", "Limited casco covers, among other things, fire, theft, storm and windscreen damage, but not damage from a collision of one's own making."], ["Yes, limited casco covers all damage to one's own car for which no counterparty can be claimed against.", "Limited casco covers only the named events; a collision caused by one's own fault is not part of it."], ["Yes, the WA insurance covers the damage, because the pillar belongs to someone else.", "The WA insurance covers the damage to the pillar of the car park's owner, not the damage to his own car."], ["Only if he reported the damage to the police within 24 hours.", "A police report is not a condition for cover of collision damage, and limited casco does not cover this damage."]], "1d.2"],
["An insured reports a claim to his car insurer. The damage is fully recovered from the liable counterparty. What does this usually mean for his no-claim bonus-malus step?", [["He does not drop back, because the damage was fully recovered.", "Bonus-malus schemes usually disregard claims that have been fully recovered."], ["He drops back as many steps as with damage caused by his own fault.", "A fully recovered claim costs the insurer nothing in the end and does not as a rule lead to a drop."], ["He loses all his accumulated no-claim years at once.", "Complete loss of no-claim years does not go with a fully recovered claim."], ["He drops back one step, because every reported claim counts.", "A fully recovered claim costs the insurer nothing in the end and as a rule does not count."]], "1d.11"],
["Bas has seven no-claim years and is high on the bonus-malus ladder. While driving off he hits his neighbour's car; the damage to that car is €400 and Bas is liable. According to his policy, a claim would make him drop back several steps. As a result his premium would rise considerably over the coming years. Which advice fits best?", [["Have it calculated whether it is cheaper to pay the damage himself or to buy back the bonus-malus step after payment.", "With small claims, paying yourself or buying back can be cheaper than a higher premium for years; many insurers offer that possibility."], ["Agree with the neighbour that he reports the damage to his car under his own WA insurance, with no consequences for Bas.", "The neighbour's WA insurance covers only damage the neighbour causes to others, not damage to his own car."], ["Switch insurer immediately, so that the claim does not count.", "Through the cancellation certificate and Roy-data, the claims history, including this claim, passes to the new insurer."], ["Agree a higher excess after the damage, so that the damage falls below it.", "Changing the policy after the damage has no influence on the handling of a claim that has already occurred."]], "1d.11"],
["How does a new car insurer know how many no-claim years a switching customer has accumulated?", [["Through the central register of no-claim years (Roy-data).", "Insurers register no-claim years centrally, so that the new insurer can check them."], ["Solely through the customer's statement on the application form.", "The customer's statement is verified against the central register."], ["Through a statement from the RDW based on the vehicle registration.", "The RDW registers vehicle registration numbers and the insurance obligation, but not accumulated no-claim years."], ["Through the Tax and Customs Administration (Belastingdienst), which keeps the claims history of cars.", "The Belastingdienst does not keep a claims history of car insurances."]], "1d.11"],
["A customer buys a speed pedelec (electric bicycle with pedal assistance up to around 45 km/h). What applies for insurance?", [["It is a registered moped and falls under the WAM insurance obligation.", "Speed pedelecs count as a moped; the WAM obligation applies to mopeds."], ["Damage to third parties falls under the AVP, as with an ordinary bicycle or e-bike.", "The AVP excludes motor vehicles such as mopeds; a WA insurance is needed for the speed pedelec."], ["Insurance is not needed, because the rider has to keep pedalling himself.", "That is true for an ordinary e-bike up to 25 km/h, but a speed pedelec is legally a moped."], ["Only a casco insurance is legally compulsory for this vehicle.", "The legal obligation concerns liability (WA), not casco."]], "1f.2"],
["What is the rebuilding value of a home?", [["The costs of rebuilding the home after damage in the same place and with the same use.", "The rebuilding value is the basis for the sum insured of a buildings insurance."], ["The market value that the owner would receive on sale of the home including land.", "Market value includes, among other things, the value of the land and the location; these are not rebuilt."], ["The WOZ value that the municipality sets annually for the property tax.", "The WOZ value is a tax value including land and says little about the rebuilding costs."], ["The forced-sale value that a valuer determines for the mortgage lender.", "The forced-sale value is a value on a compulsory sale for the lender, not the cost of rebuilding."]], "1d.4"],
["What does a guarantee against underinsurance entail for a home insurance?", [["No underinsurance, provided the value was determined by the prescribed method and changes were reported.", "The guarantee gives certainty of full reimbursement, but is subject to conditions such as a rebuilding-value calculator or appraisal and the reporting of renovations."], ["In the event of damage the full sum insured is always paid, regardless of the actual extent of the damage.", "The guarantee prevents underinsurance, but leaves the indemnity principle intact."], ["The customer never has to notify the insurer of renovations and extensions to the home again.", "Major renovations or extensions must in fact be reported in order to retain the guarantee."], ["The insurer guarantees that the premium will not rise during the term, even with indexation.", "The guarantee concerns reimbursement in the event of damage, not premium stability."]], "1f.1"],
["After a fire, the owner decides not to rebuild his home. What do home insurance policies usually provide here for the compensation?", [["Compensation based on the sale value without land, if lower than the rebuilding value.", "Whoever does not rebuild has no rebuilding costs; compensation on sale value prevents enrichment."], ["Compensation of the full rebuilding value, because the premium is always based on that value.", "If the owner does not rebuild, the full rebuilding value could lead to enrichment; policies restrict that."], ["No compensation, because the insured does not comply with his duty to rebuild.", "There is damage even without rebuilding; it is compensated, only on a different basis."], ["Compensation of the WOZ value including land, because the home has been lost.", "Land is not lost in a fire and is not compensated."]], "1h.20"],
["What is the difference between replacement value and current value for home contents?", [["Replacement value: price of new equivalent items; current value: replacement value minus wear and ageing.", "These are established concepts; policies determine when compensation is paid on current value, for example for heavily aged items."], ["Current value: the original purchase price; replacement value: the current selling price on a second-hand market.", "Current value takes wear and ageing into account; replacement value is the price of new comparable items."], ["Replacement value applies only to art and antiques; current value applies to all other contents.", "For art and antiques, replacement value or appraised value is in fact often used."], ["There is no difference for items younger than ten years; after that current value automatically and always applies.", "Young items also wear; the difference does not depend on a fixed age limit."]], "1d.4"],
["Which event usually falls under the term 'fire' in a home or home contents policy?", [["A fire with flames outside a hearth that can spread by itself.", "This is the usual definition; without flames and independent spread it is not fire."], ["A smouldering pile of wet clothes that gives off a lot of smoke but no flames.", "Smouldering without flames does not meet the definition of fire."], ["A table top that is scorched because a hot pan was put on it.", "Scorching and singeing are expressly not fire in policies."], ["A plastic container that has melted from the heat next to the stove.", "Melting without flames does not count as fire."]], "1d.2"],
["From what wind speed do most home and home contents policies speak of 'storm'?", [["At least 14 metres per second (wind force 7).", "This is the usual definition in Dutch policies; the insured can use data from the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute (KNMI)."], ["With any gust of wind that causes visible damage.", "Policies in fact state a minimum wind speed."], ["Only from wind force 12, that is hurricane force.", "That would greatly restrict the cover; the usual limit is wind force 7."], ["Only if the KNMI has issued a code red.", "A weather warning is not a condition of cover; the wind speed is decisive."]], "1d.2"],
["After a theft from a home, the insurer states that there is no cover. What condition do many home contents policies set for theft?", [["That there are signs of forced entry when entering the home.", "Many policies require signs of forced entry; without signs, theft is hard to distinguish from loss or fraud."], ["That the police arrested the offender within a month.", "Tracing the offender is not a condition of cover."], ["That the insured himself was at home at the time of the theft.", "Theft during absence is in fact the classic covered situation."], ["That the stolen items are younger than five years on the date of the damage.", "The age of the items determines the valuation, not whether there is cover."]], "1d.3"],
["Which water damage is usually covered in a standard home insurance policy?", [["Water that suddenly flows from a pipe or connected appliance because of a defect.", "Water from pipes is the classic covered water peril."], ["Water that enters the cellar through slowly rising groundwater after a wet period.", "Groundwater is usually excluded; it is not a sudden event."], ["Damp that arises from prolonged overdue maintenance of the home's roof.", "Poor maintenance is a standard exclusion."], ["Rising damp in the walls that has caused mould over the years.", "Rising damp is a gradual process and is not covered."]], "1d.2"],
["Yara rents a family home from a housing association. With the landlord's permission she has a new kitchen and a wooden floor installed at her own expense. Together this costs her well over €15,000. She asks her adviser how these improvements are insured against, for example, fire. What is the correct answer?", [["As tenant's interest on her own home contents insurance, paying attention to the maximum.", "Improvements that a tenant makes at her own expense usually fall under tenant's interest on the home contents policy; in most policies a maximum applies."], ["Through the housing association's buildings insurance, because the kitchen is fixed to the building.", "The landlord insures his own interest; the tenant's investment is usually not covered by it."], ["That is not necessary, because after installation the kitchen legally becomes the landlord's property.", "Even if the kitchen legally belongs to the home, Yara has a financial interest that she must insure herself."], ["Only through a separate buildings insurance that she takes out herself as tenant.", "A tenant does not take out a buildings insurance for the home; her interest is covered as tenant's interest on the home contents policy."]], "1c.3"],
["A customer buys an apartment in a building with an Owners' Association (Vereniging van Eigenaars, VvE). What is usual for the buildings insurance?", [["The VvE insures the building; the owner insures his contents and own improvements.", "The buildings cover of an apartment complex is usually insured by the VvE; the owner arranges the rest himself."], ["Each owner takes out his own buildings insurance for the entire building.", "That would lead to double cover; the VvE insures the building."], ["An apartment does not need to be insured, because the VvE has a reserve fund.", "The building does have to be insured, but this is done through the VvE."], ["The municipality insures the building, because it is a stacked construction.", "The municipality has no role in the insurance of private buildings."]], "1c.4"],
["The Ramdin family took out a home contents insurance with a guarantee against underinsurance eight years ago, based on a contents-value calculator. Since then the family has grown from two to five people and they have moved to a larger home. Mrs Ramdin has also inherited a collection of jewellery. The insured amount was never adjusted. What is the best advice?", [["Complete a new contents-value calculator and check the jewellery against the maximum in the policy.", "Family size, home and special possessions determine the value of the contents; such changes call for a new calculator, and in most policies separate maxima apply to jewellery."], ["Do nothing, because the guarantee against underinsurance remains in force regardless.", "In most policies the guarantee applies only as long as the calculator is up to date; after major changes it must be completed again."], ["Only have the annual indexation applied; it keeps the value up to date automatically.", "Indexation follows price developments, but not changes in family situation, home or additional possessions."], ["Only have the jewellery appraised and leave the rest of the contents unchanged.", "An appraisal of the jewellery can be useful, but the move and the growth of the family also call for a new calculator."]], "1g.2"],
["The Smit family is leaving for Australia for a year for Mr Smit's work. Their owner-occupied home in Utrecht is left empty with the contents. The neighbours look in now and then to check that everything is in order. The family wants to simply keep the home and home contents insurance running. What is the appropriate advice?", [["Inform the insurer beforehand, because vacancy limits the cover in most policies.", "Long-term vacancy increases the risk; in most policies it must be reported and otherwise the cover may be limited, for example for theft or vandalism."], ["Do nothing, because the policies continue unchanged as long as the premium is paid.", "Paying the premium is not enough; vacancy is a change in risk that must be reported under most policies."], ["Cancel the buildings insurance, because no one is living in the home temporarily.", "An empty home can also burn down or suffer storm damage; cancelling leaves the family uninsured."], ["Place the contents under a travel insurance, which also covers items at home.", "A travel insurance covers luggage while travelling, not the contents left behind at home."]], "1d.3"],
["Why do home contents policies often have a separate maximum for personal jewellery and other valuables?", [["Because they are small, valuable and prone to theft.", "A maximum limits the risk for the insurer; whoever has more can take out separate valuables cover."], ["Because the law excludes jewellery from home contents cover.", "The maxima follow from the policy, not from the law."], ["Because jewellery legally does not belong to home contents.", "Jewellery belongs to the contents; only the compensation is limited."], ["Because otherwise the premium would fall under insurance premium tax.", "Insurance premium tax is independent of the maxima for jewellery in the policy."]], "1d.3"],
["What liability does a private liability insurance (AVP) cover?", [["Liability as a private individual, not in the practice of a profession or business.", "The AVP is intended for private life; professional and business liability requires a different insurance."], ["All liability, so also when working as a self-employed entrepreneur.", "Liability as an entrepreneur falls outside the AVP."], ["Damage that the insured accidentally causes to his own belongings.", "An AVP covers damage to others, not one's own damage."], ["Damage to third parties caused with the insured's own car or motorcycle.", "Damage caused with motor vehicles is excluded; the WA insurance serves that purpose."]], "1c.4"],
["What are the requirements for liability arising from a wrongful act?", [["Unlawfulness, attributability, damage, causality and relativity.", "All requirements must be met before someone is liable on the basis of a wrongful act."], ["Damage and a valid liability insurance held by the party who caused it.", "An insurance is not a requirement for liability; the statutory requirements are much more extensive."], ["Intent of the offender and a final criminal conviction.", "Negligence can also be unlawful; a criminal conviction is not needed."], ["A written agreement between the parties and a notice of default.", "Liability arising from a wrongful act exists precisely without an agreement; a notice of default belongs to breach of contract."]], "1f.6"],
["Thijs (9 years old) is playing with friends in the street. While throwing pebbles at a tin can, he accidentally hits the neighbours' window, which breaks. The neighbours send the bill of €600 to his parents. The parents think they cannot be blamed, because they had just warned Thijs. What applies?", [["The parents are liable, even without fault of their own; their AVP may cover the damage.", "For children under 14, parents bear strict liability; they cannot free themselves by claiming they cannot be blamed. The AVP covers this liability."], ["The parents are not liable, because they had expressly warned Thijs beforehand.", "For children under 14, parents cannot free themselves by proving that they cannot be blamed."], ["Thijs is liable himself; the parents pay only if he has no money.", "A child under 14 cannot himself be held liable for a wrongful act."], ["Nobody is liable, because a 9-year-old child does not yet know what he is doing.", "The law in fact places liability for young children on the parents, so that the injured party is not left empty-handed."]], "1h.28"],
["A 15-year-old boy causes damage to another person. What applies to the liability of his parents?", [["They are liable, unless it cannot be held against them that they did not prevent it.", "For children aged 14 and 15, parents have fault-based liability with a reversed burden of proof."], ["They are always liable, without any possibility of rebuttal.", "Strict liability applies only to children under 14."], ["They are never liable; only the boy himself can be held liable.", "The boy can be liable himself, but the parents can be liable as well."], ["They are liable only if the boy still lives with them and has no income.", "Living at home or income is not the statutory criterion; the rule depends on age and blameworthiness."]], "1h.28"],
["Mrs Visser walks her dog, a calm Labrador, on a lead. When a cyclist passes, the dog is startled and bites the cyclist in the leg. Mrs Visser thinks she cannot be blamed, because the dog has never bitten before. The cyclist claims compensation for his medical costs and for pain and suffering. What applies to her liability?", [["She is strictly liable as keeper, even without fault; her AVP in principle covers this.", "For damage caused by animals the keeper bears strict liability; the fact that the animal never bit before makes no difference."], ["She is not liable, because the dog was properly on the lead and has never bitten before.", "Strict liability does not require fault; even a well-trained animal can give rise to liability."], ["She is liable only if the cyclist proves that she was careless.", "That would be fault-based liability; for animals the keeper bears strict liability."], ["Only the medical costs qualify for compensation, not pain and suffering.", "In the case of injury there is also a right to compensation for non-material damage; the AVP in principle covers that too."]], "1d.13"],
["Sanne studies in Groningen and borrows her friend Noor's laptop for an exam week. At home she accidentally knocks a glass of water over the laptop. It is irreparably damaged. Sanne has an AVP and asks her adviser whether Noor's damage will be reimbursed. What is the most important point of attention?", [["Damage to borrowed items (in her custody) is excluded or covered only to a limited extent in many AVP policies.", "Items that an insured has in her custody fall under the custody clause; in many policies there is then no cover or only limited cover."], ["The AVP always covers the damage in full, because Sanne as borrower is liable for the laptop.", "Liability alone is not enough; the policy may exclude or limit damage to items in custody."], ["Noor must first claim the damage under her own home contents insurance.", "Noor's own insurance may provide cover, but that is not a condition for a claim under Sanne's AVP."], ["The damage falls under Noor's AVP, because it concerns her own laptop.", "An AVP covers the liability of the insured herself, not damage to Noor's own belongings."]], "1h.2"],
["A group of young people deliberately wrecks a bus shelter. Tim belonged to the group but did not take part himself and had no intent. He is held liable on the basis of group liability. What do many AVP policies provide about this?", [["Tim may have cover; the insureds who acted with intent have no cover.", "Many policies limit the intent exclusion to the insured who himself had intent."], ["All group members have full cover, even those who themselves had intent.", "Whoever himself had intent is excluded from cover."], ["Nobody in the group has cover, because the damage was deliberate.", "Many policies in fact provide cover for an insured without intent of his own."], ["Tim has cover only if the police do not draw up an official report against him.", "An official police report is not a criterion for cover."]], "1h.2"],
["What is the relative limitation period for a claim for damages arising from a wrongful act?", [["Five years after becoming aware of the damage and of the liable person.", "In addition an absolute period applies; the five-year period runs from awareness."], ["Three years after the day on which the damage arose, regardless of awareness.", "Three years is the period for claims against an insurer; for a wrongful act awareness also counts."], ["One year after the day on which the injured party discovered the damage.", "A one-year period does not apply to a wrongful act."], ["Ten years after the day on which the damage-causing event took place.", "A ten-year period does not apply as a relative period; the relative period is five years from awareness."]], "1h.26"],
["What is free choice of lawyer under a legal expenses insurance?", [["The right to choose a lawyer or other qualified legal representative oneself in proceedings.", "This right follows from European law; the insurer may, however, limit the costs within reasonable bounds."], ["The right to engage one's own lawyer at the insurer's expense immediately for every legal problem.", "The free choice applies to proceedings and in conflicts of interest; outside those, the insurer may deploy its own lawyers."], ["The right of the insurer to designate the counterparty's lawyer in proceedings.", "The insurer has no say over the counterparty's lawyer."], ["The right to always have the costs of the chosen lawyer reimbursed without limit.", "Free choice does not mean unlimited reimbursement; a reasonable cost maximum is permitted."]], "1d.15"],
["Peter de Groot has his bathroom renovated by a contractor in March. Already in April a dispute arises about leaks and the quality of the tiling. In June Peter takes out a legal expenses insurance with the housing module. In August he asks the insurer for help with the dispute with the contractor. What is the insurer's likely answer?", [["No cover, because the dispute already existed before the insurance was concluded.", "Legal expenses insurances do not cover conflicts that already existed or were foreseeable at inception; there must be uncertainty."], ["Cover, because the waiting period in the policy has by now long expired.", "Even after a waiting period has expired, a dispute that already existed before conclusion remains excluded."], ["Cover, but only for the costs of an external lawyer.", "Whether there is cover does not depend on the type of legal representative; the dispute already existed before the start date."], ["Cover, provided Peter pays an additional excess.", "An existing dispute cannot be insured by paying a higher excess."]], "1d.3"],
["What is the purpose of a waiting period in a legal expenses insurance?", [["To prevent someone from insuring when a conflict is already looming.", "Disputes arising during the waiting period are not covered; this keeps the insurance affordable for everyone."], ["To give the insurer time to find a suitable lawyer.", "The waiting period concerns the start date of the cover, not the handling."], ["To collect the premium only after the waiting period has expired.", "Premium is due from the start date, including during the waiting period."], ["To give the customer the chance to cancel the policy free of charge.", "Cancelling free of charge belongs to a cooling-off period, not to a waiting period."]], "1d.15"],
["The insured and the legal expenses insurer disagree about the feasibility of proceedings. What does the dispute procedure in most policies offer?", [["An opinion from an external lawyer of the insured's choice, at the insurer's expense.", "The dispute procedure ensures an independent opinion on the approach to the case."], ["A compulsory referral to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) before the case can be dealt with further on its merits.", "Kifid deals with complaints, not with the substantive approach; the dispute procedure is part of the policy."], ["A final decision by the insurer itself, without further review.", "The dispute procedure exists precisely to prevent that."], ["Court proceedings, the costs of which the insured advances himself.", "The dispute procedure is an internal arrangement with an external lawyer's opinion, not court proceedings at one's own expense."]], "1h.29"],
["Which reason for cancellation is usually covered under a cancellation insurance?", [["Serious illness or death of a close relative before departure.", "Cancellation insurances cover only unforeseen events named in the policy; serious illness and death are core reasons."], ["No longer wanting to go on the trip because of a change of plans.", "A choice of one's own is not an unforeseen event and so is not covered."], ["A cheaper offer for the same trip from another agency.", "A price difference is not an insured reason for cancellation."], ["Bad weather forecasts for the period of the trip.", "Bad weather is not a covered reason for cancellation."]], "1d.14"],
["How does the cover for medical costs in a travel insurance relate to the basic health insurance (Zvw)?", [["It supplements it: it covers costs abroad that the health insurance does not reimburse.", "A health insurance often does not reimburse everything abroad; the travel insurance covers the difference, depending on the conditions."], ["It fully replaces the health insurance as long as the insured is travelling.", "The health insurance continues to apply; the travel insurance supplements it."], ["It reimburses only healthcare costs incurred after returning to the Netherlands.", "A travel insurance is in fact intended for costs during a trip, including abroad."], ["It is legally compulsory for everyone travelling outside Europe.", "A travel insurance is voluntary, including for trips outside Europe."]], "1h.31"],
["On what basis does a travel insurance usually reimburse stolen luggage?", [["On current value, with maxima per category and an excess.", "Travel insurances usually work with current value and limits, for example for electronics and jewellery."], ["Always on replacement value, without a maximum.", "Unlimited replacement-value compensation is unusual for luggage."], ["On the basis of the purchase price, even if that was decades ago.", "The purchase price does not take wear and ageing into account."], ["On the basis of a fixed amount per suitcase.", "Compensation depends on the actual value, not on the number of suitcases."]], "1h.10"],
["A holiday area is hit by a natural disaster shortly before departure. Which arrangement can help with a package holiday from an affiliated tour operator?", [["The Travel Calamities Fund (Calamiteitenfonds Reizen), which covers calamities at the destination.", "The Calamities Fund covers calamities at the destination for affiliated tour operators, something that cancellation insurances often exclude."], ["The Travel Guarantee Fund Foundation (SGR), which also compensates natural disasters.", "The SGR protects in the event of the tour operator's insolvency, not in the event of calamities."], ["Kifid, which pays travellers a contribution in the event of disasters.", "Kifid deals with complaints and does not itself pay out any damage."], ["The Guarantee Fund, which absorbs damage caused by unforeseen disasters.", "The Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund concerns traffic damage involving uninsured or unknown vehicles."]], "1d.14"],
["The Hoekstra family has a seven-seater car. However, the passenger accident insurance (OVI) was insured for five seats when it was taken out. One Sunday they drive to grandpa and grandma with seven people. In a collision several occupants suffer permanent injury. What do most OVI policies provide if there are more occupants than insured seats?", [["The payment per occupant is reduced proportionally, here to five sevenths.", "In most OVI policies the sum insured is linked to the number of insured seats; with more occupants the payment is reduced proportionally."], ["Nothing at all is paid, because there were too many occupants.", "Complete lapse of cover goes too far; most policies reduce the payment proportionally."], ["Only the five occupants with the most serious injury receive the full amount.", "Policies do not select on the severity of the injury; all occupants receive a proportionate share."], ["Every occupant receives the full amount; the number of seats counts only for the premium.", "The number of insured seats in fact determines the maximum payment; with more occupants it is reduced proportionally."]], "1d.9"],
["Why is a passenger damage insurance (SVI) seen by most customers as a better alternative to a passenger accident insurance (OVI)?", [["The SVI compensates the actual damage; the OVI pays only fixed amounts on death or disability.", "With an SVI the actual personal injury and property damage of occupants is compensated, including for example loss of income; an OVI covers only predetermined amounts."], ["The SVI always pays in addition to other insurances, whereas the OVI is set off against other payments.", "It is the other way round: the OVI is a fixed-sum insurance that pays in addition to other payments; the SVI, as a non-life insurance, compensates only the actual damage."], ["The SVI also covers damage to the car itself, so that a casco insurance becomes unnecessary.", "The SVI is not a casco insurance; damage to the car itself falls outside it."], ["The SVI also applies to accidents outside the car, such as on a bicycle or as a pedestrian.", "The SVI is linked to the insured motor vehicle and does not apply to accidents as a cyclist or pedestrian."]], "1d.9"],
["Rob has a 9-metre sailing boat with a casco insurance for pleasure craft. In the policy the cruising area is limited to Dutch inland waters and the Wadden Sea. This summer he sails with friends across the North Sea to England. Off the English coast, the boat runs aground on a sandbank in bad weather and is badly damaged. What is the main cover problem?", [["The damage arose outside the insured cruising area and is therefore in principle not covered.", "The cruising area determines where the cover applies; whoever sails outside it without an extension has no cover under most policies."], ["Running aground is always excluded, even within the insured cruising area.", "Running aground is in fact a covered event in most casco policies; the problem is the cruising area."], ["A casco insurance for a sailing boat covers only fire and theft.", "A casco policy for pleasure craft covers much more, such as storm, collision and running aground."], ["For the crossing to England, Rob would only have needed a green card.", "The green card belongs to motor vehicles; for a boat it is the cruising area in the policy that matters."]], "1c.10"],
["Which information document must a customer receive under the IDD before a non-life insurance is concluded?", [["The standardised information document (IPID).", "The IPID gives, in a fixed layout, the main features, covers and exclusions of a non-life insurance."], ["A key information document (KID) under PRIIPs.", "The KID is intended for packaged investment products, not for non-life insurances."], ["A European Standardised Information Sheet (ESIS).", "The ESIS belongs to mortgage credit."], ["An annual statement with the premium and the covers.", "An annual statement is not a pre-contractual product document."]], "1e.1"],
["Under the IDD, must a distributor also determine the customer's demands and needs when selling without advice (execution-only)?", [["Yes, every sale must fit the customer's demands and needs.", "The IDD requires demands and needs to be determined for every distribution, even without advice."], ["No, with execution-only the customer is entirely responsible himself.", "The demands-and-needs test also applies to sales without advice."], ["Only if the insured amount is above a statutory limit.", "The IDD makes no distinction by insured amount."], ["Only for life insurances, not for non-life insurances.", "The obligation applies to all insurances, including non-life insurances."]], "1e.1"],
["What information about his remuneration must an intermediary give under the IDD before a non-life insurance is concluded, in any case?", [["The nature of the remuneration: commission, fee or a combination.", "The IDD requires transparency about the nature of the remuneration, so that the customer sees possible conflicts of interest."], ["The adviser's annual income over the past year.", "The adviser's income is not part of the mandatory information."], ["The insurer's profit margin on the product concerned.", "The intermediary does not have to provide that information."], ["Nothing, because remuneration information is mandatory only for life insurance.", "Information about the nature of the remuneration is also mandatory for non-life insurances."]], "1g.1"],
["May an adviser receive commission for intermediation in a personal home contents insurance?", [["Yes, the commission ban does not cover this non-life insurance.", "The commission ban applies to complex products and high-impact products such as mortgages; non-life insurances do not fall under it."], ["No, commission has been prohibited for all insurances since 2013.", "The commission ban of 2013 does not apply to non-life insurances."], ["Only if the customer has asked for it in writing beforehand.", "There is no statutory requirement for the customer to ask for commission beforehand."], ["Only if the premium is below a statutory amount.", "The commission ban has no premium limit; it depends on the type of product."]], "1g.1"],
["An insurer offers a home contents insurance as a package together with an AVP. What information must the customer then receive under the IDD?", [["Whether the components can also be bought separately, and if so, what they cost separately.", "In the case of bundled sales the IDD requires information about the possibility of buying separately and the cost of each component."], ["An overview of all comparable packages from competing insurers in the market.", "A market overview of competitors is not an IDD obligation for bundled sales."], ["A written approval from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) for the combination of products.", "The AFM does not approve packages in advance."], ["A calculation of the profit the insurer makes on the package.", "The insurer's profit margin does not have to be provided."]], "1g.1"],
["Marieke wants to take out a continuous travel insurance with cancellation cover through her adviser. During the inventory it turns out that she has a credit card with a travel insurance. Her health insurance also reimburses emergency care abroad. She travels three times a year, mostly within Europe. What best fits the adviser's duty of care?", [["Analyse the existing covers and point out to Marieke any overlap and gaps.", "Good advice starts with an inventory of existing covers; double cover costs premium unnecessarily and gaps must be identified."], ["Take out the travel insurance without further questions, because the customer expressly asks for it herself.", "Even with a specific request, the adviser must check whether the product fits the customer's demands and needs."], ["Have the credit card cover cancelled, because it is always worse.", "Credit card covers differ greatly; without analysis it cannot be said whether it is worse."], ["Advise against taking a travel insurance, because the health insurance already covers everything.", "The health insurance covers only medical costs, and often not in full; luggage, cancellation and repatriation fall outside it."]], "1d.5"],
["Johan (58) wants to switch to another health insurer on 1 January and also take out a comprehensive supplementary dental insurance there. He has poor teeth and expects to need several implants soon. For the supplementary dental insurance he has to complete a questionnaire about his teeth. He finds that strange, because he did not have to fill in anything for the basic insurance. What is the explanation?", [["No duty of acceptance applies to the supplementary insurance; the insurer may assess the risk.", "A duty of acceptance applies to the basic insurance; for supplementary insurances the insurer may ask questions, attach conditions or refuse. Johan must answer the questions truthfully."], ["The questionnaire is not permitted, because supplementary insurances also have a duty of acceptance.", "The statutory duty of acceptance applies only to the basic insurance, not to supplementary insurances."], ["The questions are meant only to be able to recommend a dentist in his region.", "The questions serve precisely to assess the risk and may affect acceptance."], ["The insurer may ask questions, but must always accept him on the same conditions.", "Without a duty of acceptance the insurer may also impose restrictive conditions or refuse the application."]], "1c.17"],
["What is usual when an insured disagrees with the damage assessment by the insurer's expert?", [["Engage his own counter-expert, with reimbursement of costs up to the level of the insurer's expert.", "Policies usually offer this arrangement; above that amount only if the costs are reasonable."], ["Simply accept the damage assessment, because the insurer's expert decides in a binding way.", "The insured may engage a counter-expert; the insurer's expert does not decide in a binding way."], ["Start court proceedings immediately, because a counter-expert opinion is not permitted.", "A counter-expert opinion is in fact usual and permitted before a dispute goes to court."], ["File a complaint with the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM), which then re-determines the extent of the damage.", "The AFM does not deal with individual damage disputes and does not determine damage."]], "1h.1"],
["What is an important requirement for a loss adjuster engaged by the insurer?", [["He must assess the damage independently and expertly.", "The Netherlands Institute of Registered Experts (NIVRE) sets requirements for professional competence and conduct."], ["He must be employed by the insured to safeguard the insured's interests.", "The insurer's expert works on behalf of the insurer; the insured can engage a counter-expert himself."], ["He must assess the damage as low as possible for the insurer.", "An expert must assess the damage objectively, not as low as possible."], ["He must also determine the policy's new premium for every claim.", "The premium is not a task of the loss adjuster."]], "1h.6"],
["How is the burden of proof usually divided in an insurance claim?", [["Insured: make the covered event plausible; insurer: prove an exclusion.", "Whoever invokes a right must prove it; an exclusion is a defence of the insurer."], ["The insurer must in all cases prove that no damage has arisen.", "The insured must first make his claim plausible."], ["The insured must show that no exclusion in the policy applies.", "The insurer must prove its reliance on an exclusion."], ["The loss adjuster bears the full burden of proof for both cover and exclusion.", "The expert determines the damage, but is not a party to proceedings and bears no burden of proof."]], "1h.13"],
["What must a consumer do before he can submit a complaint about his insurer to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute)?", [["First complain to the insurer itself and await its position.", "Kifid deals with a complaint only after the internal complaints procedure has been completed."], ["First start proceedings before the subdistrict court and await the judgment.", "Kifid is in fact an alternative to the court; a judgment is not needed."], ["First ask the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) for permission to submit the complaint.", "The AFM plays no role in access to Kifid."], ["First cancel the insurance, so that there is no longer an ongoing relationship.", "Cancellation is not a condition for a complaint to Kifid."]], "1h.9"],
["Who determines which care is in the basic package of the health insurance and how high the mandatory deductible is?", [["The government, through legislation and regulations, with advice from, among others, the National Health Care Institute (Zorginstituut Nederland).", "The content of the basic package and the mandatory deductible are set politically; health insurers may not deviate from them."], ["Each health insurer itself, within the limits of the Health Insurance Act (Zvw).", "Insurers determine their premium and which care they purchase, but not the content of the basic package or the mandatory deductible."], ["De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB), as supervisor of the health insurers.", "DNB exercises prudential supervision of insurers, but does not set the package or the deductible."], ["The Dutch Healthcare Authority (NZa), which also sets the premiums of the basic insurance.", "The NZa supervises the healthcare market, but the premiums are set by the health insurers themselves."]], "1e.2"],
["What can Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) NOT do?", [["Impose fines on financial service providers.", "Fines are a power of supervisors such as the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM); Kifid settles disputes."], ["Give a ruling in a dispute between a consumer and an insurer.", "That is the core task of the Disputes Committee."], ["Mediate between consumer and insurer.", "Kifid often tries to mediate first."], ["Hear a dispute on appeal via the Appeals Committee.", "The Appeals Committee is part of Kifid."]], "1h.9"],
["What is the main function of Stichting CIS (the CIS Foundation)?", [["Managing central databases for risk assessment and fraud control.", "Insurers consult CIS at acceptance and when handling claims, within the privacy rules."], ["Dealing with consumer complaints about insurers in a binding way.", "Complaints are dealt with by the insurer and then by Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute)."], ["Supervising the solvency of personal non-life and life insurers.", "Prudential supervision lies with De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB)."], ["Reinsuring terrorism damage for affiliated insurers.", "That is the role of the terrorism reinsurer."]], "1a.10"],
["A consumer wants to know what data about him is registered with Stichting CIS (the CIS Foundation). What can he do?", [["Submit an access request to CIS under the GDPR (AVG).", "Under the GDPR (AVG) everyone has the right to know which personal data about him is being processed."], ["Nothing; CIS data is secret from consumers.", "The GDPR (AVG) does give a right of access."], ["Submit a request to the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM).", "The AFM does not manage the CIS data."], ["Start a lawsuit, because access is possible only through the court.", "Access is possible without a court; an access request to CIS is the appropriate route."]], "1a.10"],
["What is the role of the joint reinsurer for terrorism damage in the Netherlands?", [["It reinsures terrorism damage for insurers, up to an annual maximum.", "Through the terrorism reinsurer, insurers can cover terrorism damage; the payment is determined according to the payment protocol."], ["It pays terrorism damage directly to uninsured consumers and businesses.", "The terrorism reinsurer pays insurers, not uninsured consumers or businesses directly."], ["It supervises the solvency of insurers on behalf of De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB).", "Supervision lies with the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) and DNB; this body reinsures only terrorism damage."], ["It reinsures flood damage caused by the failure of dykes.", "Flooding is a different risk; the terrorism reinsurer concerns terrorism."]], "1h.2"],
["Which forms of armed conflict and unrest ('molest') are excluded as standard in Dutch policies?", [["Armed conflict, civil war, insurrection, civil disturbance, riot and mutiny.", "These are the six fixed forms of molest according to the usual insurers' definition."], ["Terrorism, malicious contamination, sabotage, hostage-taking, kidnapping and cyber attacks.", "Terrorism and related risks fall under the terrorism clause and the terrorism reinsurer, not under the molest definition."], ["Burglary, theft, vandalism, robbery, extortion and arson by third parties.", "These are ordinary perils (covered or not), not molest."], ["Storm, hail, flooding, earthquake, volcanic eruption and lightning strike.", "These are natural perils, not molest."]], "1h.2"],
["Which flood damage is usually NOT covered by home and home contents policies in the Netherlands?", [["Damage caused by the failure of a primary flood defence, such as a sea dyke.", "This catastrophe risk is usually excluded; flooding through a non-primary (regional) defence is covered in many policies."], ["Damage caused by heavy rain unexpectedly flowing into the home through the roof.", "Precipitation damage is in fact covered in many policies."], ["Damage caused by a burst water pipe in the home during frost.", "Water from pipes is a peril covered as standard."], ["Damage caused by the failure of a small regional defence along a polder.", "Many policies do cover this nowadays."]], "1h.2"],
["What applies to compensation under the Disaster Damage Compensation Act (Wts)?", [["There is no compensation for damage that could reasonably be insured.", "The Wts is a safety net for uninsurable damage and is declared applicable per disaster."], ["The Wts compensates all damage, even if the customer could have taken out insurance.", "Insurable damage is in fact excluded."], ["The Wts is a compulsory insurance for homeowners.", "The Wts is a government scheme, not an insurance."], ["The Wts applies automatically to every storm.", "The Wts must be declared applicable per disaster."]], "1c.1"],
["What does 'repair takes precedence over replacement' mean in sustainable claims handling?", [["Repairing where possible instead of replacing, to save raw materials.", "More and more insurers steer towards repair, provided the quality is equivalent."], ["From now on the insured must always repair damaged items himself and at his own expense.", "Repair is arranged by or through the insurer and is not imposed on the insured."], ["Replacement of damaged items is no longer legally permitted.", "Replacement remains possible if repair is not sensible."], ["Repair is compulsory because it is cheaper in all cases.", "Sustainability, not cost, is the starting point."]], "1h.3"],
["Lucas (22) comes from Poland and has just started working for a distribution centre in Venlo. He lives in the Netherlands and pays wage tax here. He thinks he does not need to insure himself here, because he still has a health insurance in Poland. His employer points out the Dutch rules to him. What applies?", [["He is subject to the insurance obligation and must take out a Dutch health insurance within four months.", "Anyone who lives or works in the Netherlands and is therefore insured under the Long-term Care Act (Wlz) must take out a health insurance; within four months this can be done with retroactive effect."], ["He does not need to arrange anything, as long as his Polish health insurance simply continues.", "Because of his work in the Netherlands he falls under the Dutch social insurances; his Polish insurance does not replace the insurance obligation."], ["He must insure himself, but only after he has lived in the Netherlands for one year.", "The insurance obligation applies from the moment he lives or works here, not only after a year."], ["His employer must take out a health insurance for him and pay the premium for it.", "The insurance obligation rests on Lucas himself; the employer does pay the employer's levy under the Health Insurance Act (Zvw) on his salary."]], "1f.8"],
["Marloes has a benefits-in-kind policy for her basic insurance. She wants to have a knee operation at a private clinic that has no contract with her health insurer. Her neighbour has a reimbursement policy and says that she gets everything reimbursed at such a clinic. Marloes asks her adviser what the difference is. What is the correct answer?", [["At a non-contracted clinic, her benefits-in-kind policy may reimburse less than a reimbursement policy.", "A benefits-in-kind policy fully reimburses contracted care; for non-contracted care the insurer may give a lower reimbursement. A reimbursement policy reimburses up to the market-based rate."], ["There is no difference: both policy types fully reimburse every care provider in the Netherlands.", "With a benefits-in-kind policy the reimbursement for non-contracted care may in fact be lower."], ["Her benefits-in-kind policy does not reimburse care from a non-contracted clinic in any case.", "A benefits-in-kind policy as a rule does reimburse non-contracted care, but often only partially."], ["With a reimbursement policy the mandatory deductible does not apply to an operation in a clinic.", "The mandatory deductible applies to both policy types."]], "1c.16"],
["What are the main components from which the basic insurance under the Health Insurance Act (Zvw) is financed?", [["The nominal premium paid to the health insurer and the income-related contribution.", "The insured pays the nominal premium to his health insurer; in addition there is an income-related contribution, which for employees runs via the employer."], ["Only the premium that the insured pays directly to the health insurer himself.", "In addition to the nominal premium there is also an income-related contribution; it forms a large part of the funding."], ["The premium for the Long-term Care Act (Wlz) and the mandatory deductible.", "The Wlz premium finances long-term care; the deductible is not a premium but a personal payment when using care."], ["A fixed contribution from the employer and the healthcare allowance from the Tax and Customs Administration.", "The healthcare allowance is a contribution towards the premium for people on a lower income, not a source of funding for the premium itself."]], "1d.16"],
["In the case of personal injury, an insurer processes medical data. What is usual to safeguard privacy?", [["Assessment by a medical adviser bound by medical professional confidentiality.", "Health data is special category personal data; access is limited to the medical adviser and those who strictly need it."], ["Access by all claims handlers working on the file.", "Access to health data must be strictly limited."], ["Forwarding the full medical file to the counterparty.", "Sharing with the counterparty without necessity and consent is contrary to the GDPR (AVG)."], ["Storage in a joint database that all insurers can consult.", "Such a shared medical database is contrary to data minimisation and medical professional confidentiality."]], "1h.6"],
["In a non-life insurance, what is the difference between the policyholder and the insured?", [["The policyholder is the contracting party; the insured is the person whose interest is covered.", "The policyholder is the insurer's contracting party; the insured is the person who suffers the damage and is entitled to payment. Often this is the same person, but not always."], ["There is no difference; in Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW) both terms always denote the same person.", "That is not correct: with a family AVP, for example, the partner and children are also insured, while only one person is the policyholder."], ["The insured concludes the contract; the policyholder is the person who receives the payment after a claim.", "This reverses the roles: the policyholder is precisely the contracting party who enters into the insurance."], ["The policyholder is the intermediary who places the insurance with the insurer on behalf of the customer.", "An intermediary mediates, but is not a party to the insurance contract and therefore not a policyholder."]], "1c.5"],
["On Monday, Mr Pieters takes out a casco insurance. Both parties know that his car was already damaged in a collision on Sunday. What applies to that damage?", [["No cover, because when the contract was concluded it was already established that this damage had arisen.", "An insurance requires uncertainty about the occurrence of damage. Damage that was already certain at conclusion can no longer be insured."], ["Full cover, because the insurer knew about the damage and accepted the insurance anyway.", "The insurer's knowledge does not make certain damage insurable; the requirement of uncertainty is still lacking for this event."], ["Partial cover in proportion to the premium paid for the first day.", "A proportional payment belongs to breach of the duty of disclosure, not to damage that was already established before conclusion."], ["Cover, provided the damage is reported within fourteen days of the conclusion of the insurance.", "Timely reporting does not change the fact that at conclusion there was no longer any uncertainty about this damage."]], "1a.1"],
["How is an insurance contract formed according to the Dutch Civil Code?", [["By offer and acceptance; the policy that the insurer issues afterwards serves as evidence.", "The contract is free of form and arises as soon as the parties agree. The insurer must draw up a policy, but this is evidence and not a condition for its existence."], ["Only when the policyholder has received the policy and returned it signed.", "Signing the policy is not a statutory requirement; the contract already exists from the meeting of minds."], ["Only after payment of the first premium, because before that the contract is not legally valid.", "Payment of the premium is an obligation under the contract, not a condition for its formation."], ["Exclusively by a written deed, because the law sets a formal requirement for insurances.", "The law sets no formal requirement; an insurance can also be concluded orally, by telephone or online."]], "1a.1"],
["A policyholder does not report an earlier claims history, but the insurer already knew about it from its own records. Can the insurer rely on breach of the duty of disclosure?", [["No, the insurer cannot rely on facts that it already knew or ought to have known.", "The duty of disclosure protects the insurer against unknown risk factors. What it already knew or should have known, it cannot hold against the customer."], ["Yes, the policyholder must always report relevant facts himself, regardless of what the insurer already knows.", "The law in fact excludes reliance on breach for facts that the insurer knows or ought to know."], ["Yes, but only if the insurer has a claim within two months of the conclusion of the insurance.", "The two-month period concerns the moment at which the insurer must respond after discovery, not a restriction like this."], ["Only if the claims history arose with a different insurer than the current one.", "Where the damage arose is irrelevant; what is decisive is whether the insurer already knew or ought to have known the fact."]], "1a.8"],
["After a fire it turns out that the policyholder, without intent, failed to report an earlier arson in his home. The insurer shows that, had it been reported correctly, it would not have concluded the insurance at all. What is the consequence?", [["The insurer does not have to pay anything.", "If the insurer would not have concluded an insurance had it known the true state of affairs, it is not obliged under the law to pay, even without intent on the part of the customer."], ["The insurer pays in proportion to the premium, as with a premium that was too low.", "The proportionality rule applies if the insurer would have insured at a higher premium, not if it would not have insured at all."], ["The insurer must pay in full, because there was no intent to mislead and the premium has been paid.", "The absence of intent prevents only the most severe sanction; if the insurer would never have insured, the right to payment lapses anyway."], ["The insurer pays half, because the fault is shared between customer and insurer.", "The law has no fixed division of half; the consequences depend on what the insurer would have done had it been reported correctly."]], "1a.8"],
["After a claim the insurer shows that, had a non-reported fact been reported correctly, it would have stipulated a higher excess, at the same premium. There was no intent. What applies?", [["The insurer pays as if that higher excess had been agreed from the start.", "In the case of conditions other than a higher premium, the damage is compensated as if those conditions had applied; the customer is thus placed in the situation of a correct report."], ["The insurer pays nothing, because the policyholder breached the duty of disclosure.", "Complete refusal applies only in the case of intent to mislead or if the insurer would not have insured at all."], ["The insurer pays in full without an excess, because the premium would not have been different.", "The fact that the premium would have remained the same does not mean the customer may keep the benefit of the lower excess."], ["The insurer pays in proportion to the premium paid compared with the correct premium.", "The proportionality rule fits a higher premium; here only the excess would have been different, and then that different excess applies."]], "1a.8"],
["What in principle applies to reporting criminal offences when taking out an insurance?", [["Facts from more than eight years before conclusion need not in principle be reported.", "The law limits the duty of disclosure for criminal offences in time; old facts from more than eight years ago in principle do not have to be mentioned."], ["All criminal offences must be reported, however long ago they took place.", "The law in fact has a period of eight years, so that someone is not pursued for life by an old criminal past."], ["Criminal offences never have to be reported, because they fall under privacy legislation.", "Criminal offences may well be relevant to the risk and, within the period, fall under the duty of disclosure."], ["Only convictions with a prison sentence of more than four years have to be reported.", "No such sentence threshold exists in the duty-of-disclosure rules; the law works with a time limit of eight years."]], "1a.8"],
["An insurer includes in its conditions that any incorrect statement made on application, even without intent, leads to forfeiture of all rights. What applies to this?", [["The clause is invalid; it may not deviate to the detriment of the policyholder.", "The duty-of-disclosure rules are semi-mandatory law: deviation is permitted only in favour of the policyholder, not to his detriment."], ["This clause is valid, because the parties to a non-life insurance have complete freedom of contract.", "No complete freedom of contract applies to the duty of disclosure; the law protects the policyholder against stricter clauses."], ["This clause is valid only if it is in bold in the policy and the customer has signed separately for it.", "Prominent presentation or a signature does not make a clause that deviates from semi-mandatory law valid after all."], ["This clause is valid for consumers, but not for insurances taken out by a business.", "The opposite is more likely true: the statutory protection is intended for the policyholder, and certainly applies to consumers."]], "1a.8"],
["When, according to case law, is there recklessness within the meaning of the statutory exclusion in non-life insurances?", [["When the insured was aware of the considerable chance of damage and nevertheless took that risk.", "It concerns conscious recklessness: the insured realised the danger and accepted it. Ordinary inattentiveness does not fall under it."], ["When the insured makes a small mistake that could have been prevented in hindsight.", "A mistake or ordinary carelessness is not recklessness; otherwise almost every claim would be excluded."], ["When the insured did not report the damage to the insurer within the period stated in the policy.", "Late reporting is a breach of the duty to report with its own consequences; it says nothing about how the damage arose."], ["When the damage is greater than the policyholder had foreseen when taking out the insurance.", "The extent of the damage does not determine whether there is recklessness; it concerns the insured's behaviour and awareness."]], "1a.5"],
["Mrs Kok and her partner are both insured on a home contents policy. After an argument her partner deliberately starts a fire. Mrs Kok knew nothing about it. What is the starting point under the law?", [["Mrs Kok in principle retains her right; the statutory intent exclusion applies per insured.", "The statutory exclusion affects only the insured who caused the damage intentionally or through recklessness. Policies can, however, extend this to co-insureds."], ["Neither has a right to payment, because the law attributes the intent of one insured to all insureds.", "The law does not automatically attribute intent to other insureds; that can only follow from a provision in the policy."], ["Both are entitled to full payment, because intent cannot be excluded in a home contents insurance.", "The partner who started the fire deliberately has no right to payment under the law; the exclusion is in fact laid down by law."], ["Mrs Kok receives payment only if her partner has been convicted of the arson in criminal proceedings.", "A criminal conviction is not a condition; the insurer must make the intent plausible in civil proceedings."]], "1a.5"],
["What duty does an insured have under Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW) as soon as covered damage is imminent or has occurred?", [["To take reasonable measures, to the best of his ability, to prevent or limit the damage.", "This is the duty to mitigate. If the insured breaches it and the insurer thereby suffers a disadvantage, the payment may be reduced by that disadvantage."], ["To do nothing until the insurer's expert has looked at the damage, so that no evidence is lost.", "Waiting can in fact increase the damage; the insured must take reasonable measures immediately and can record the evidence with photos."], ["To have the damage fully repaired by a tradesman within 24 hours, so that no consequential damage arises.", "Full repair before assessment is not a statutory duty and can in fact make the damage assessment more difficult."], ["To report the damage to the police immediately, even if no criminal offence is involved.", "Reporting to the police is relevant in the case of, for example, theft or vandalism, but is not a general statutory obligation for every imminent damage."]], "1h.4"],
["During a severe storm, part of the roof tiles blow off the Bos family's home. Heavy rain is expected that night. The same evening Mr Bos has a roofer place a tarpaulin to prevent rain getting in; this costs €450. The home insurance policy covers storm damage. How are the costs of the tarpaulin treated?", [["As mitigation costs: they are reimbursed because they were incurred to limit imminent covered damage.", "The tarpaulin prevents further water damage after covered storm damage. Such reasonable costs in the case of immediate danger fall under mitigation."], ["As maintenance costs: these are always for the account of the home's owner himself.", "Maintenance is periodic care of the home; here it concerns an emergency measure after a covered event, and that is mitigation."], ["They are reimbursed only if the insurer gave prior written permission.", "The law does not require prior permission for reasonable mitigation costs; in an emergency it often cannot be obtained either."], ["They are not reimbursed, because at that moment there was no water damage to the contents yet.", "Mitigation is precisely meant to prevent damage before it arises; the fact that there was no water damage yet is no reason for refusal."]], "1h.15"],
["An injured party holds an insured liable. The AVP insurer conducts the defence on behalf of the insured in proceedings. Who bears the costs of that defence?", [["The insurer, even if as a result the sum insured is exceeded together with the compensation.", "In a liability insurance, the costs of defence incurred with the insurer's consent are for its account, if necessary above the sum insured."], ["The insured himself, because an AVP covers only the compensation to the injured party and no costs.", "The law and the policies in fact assume that the insurer conducts the defence and bears its costs."], ["The insurer, but only up to the amount remaining of the sum insured after deduction of the compensation.", "Defence costs are not limited to the remainder of the sum insured; if necessary they come on top of it."], ["The injured party, because he starts the proceedings and thus takes on the risk of the costs.", "An injured party may be ordered to pay costs if he loses, but the insurer bears the insured's own defence costs."]], "1h.16"],
["A consumer takes out a car insurance with a contract term of one year. After four months he wants to switch. What is the starting point?", [["In principle he can cancel only at the end of the first contract period, unless the policy allows more room.", "The statutory right to cancel at any time with a notice period of at most one month applies only after tacit renewal; in the first period the agreed term applies."], ["He can cancel at any time with a notice period of at most one month, even in the first year.", "That right applies to the consumer after tacit renewal of the first contract period, not automatically during that first period."], ["He can never cancel mid-term, even after renewal, because a car insurance is a compulsory insurance.", "Even compulsory WA insurances can be cancelled monthly after renewal; the customer must then be insured elsewhere."], ["He can cancel only if he pays the premium for the entire remaining term and a fine in advance.", "The law has no fine for cancellation; the only question is when cancellation is possible."]], "1a.9"],
["What does provisional cover entail?", [["Temporary cover while the application is being assessed, until the insurer decides.", "In this way the customer is already insured while the application is being assessed. If the application is refused, the cover ends, usually after a short period."], ["Cover that starts only once the insurer has assessed the application and sent the policy.", "That describes definitive cover; provisional cover exists precisely before that assessment."], ["Partial cover under which claims during the acceptance period are reimbursed by half.", "Provisional cover is not partial cover; within the agreed conditions the ordinary cover applies."], ["Cover that applies exclusively to claims that were already known to the policyholder before the application.", "Known claims cannot be insured; provisional cover concerns uncertain future events."]], "1a.6"],
["Lars has a racing bike worth €3,500. The bike is co-insured on his home contents policy and he also has a separate bicycle insurance. Both policies provide that they offer no cover if the damage is also insured elsewhere. The bike is stolen from his locked shed. What applies?", [["The clauses cancel each other out; both insurers bear the damage together.", "If both policies contain such a clause, they cancel each other out; Lars has his damage reimbursed and the insurers divide it among themselves."], ["Neither insurer pays, because each refers to the other.", "The law prevents the insured from being left empty-handed by two clauses referring to each other."], ["Only the insurer with the oldest policy pays the full damage to Lars.", "A ranking by policy start date does not apply here; the insurers bear the damage together."], ["Lars receives the value twice, because he has paid premium twice.", "The indemnity principle prohibits the insured from profiting from damage; he has his damage reimbursed once."]], "1d.5"],
["An insurance ends halfway through the premium year, for example because the insured car has been sold. What in principle applies to the premium paid in advance?", [["The policyholder receives back the premium for the remaining period.", "In the case of mid-term termination, the policyholder is in principle entitled to a refund of premium pro rata temporis, except in the case of, for example, intent to mislead."], ["The premium stays with the insurer, because it has reserved risk for a full year.", "After the end the insurer no longer runs any risk; retaining premium without anything in return is in principle not permitted."], ["The policyholder receives premium back only if he reported no claim in the current year.", "A previous claim report is in principle no reason to withhold the premium for the remaining period."], ["The premium is converted into a credit that can be used only with the same insurer.", "The policyholder is entitled to a refund; a compulsory credit with the same insurer does not follow from the law."]], "1a.9"],
["Two cars collide at a junction. On what basis is liability between the two motorists assessed?", [["On the general rules of wrongful act, that is, on the basis of fault.", "The special protection of Article 185 of the Road Traffic Act (WVW) applies to non-motorised road users; between motor vehicles ordinary fault-based liability applies."], ["On Article 185 WVW, so that the driver of the heaviest car is in principle liable.", "Article 185 WVW protects pedestrians and cyclists against motor vehicles; it does not apply between two motor vehicles."], ["Each party always bears its own damage, regardless of who caused the collision.", "Whoever causes the collision through a fault is liable; there is no general rule that everyone bears their own damage."], ["The driver coming from the right is always 50% liable because of the priority rules.", "Priority rules play a role in establishing the fault, but there is no fixed 50% liability for a particular party."]], "1h.23"],
["Lotte is a passenger in her boyfriend Ruben's car. Through Ruben's inattentiveness the car hits a stationary lorry. Lotte suffers whiplash and cannot work for several months. Ruben has only a WA insurance for his car. Through which insurance is Lotte's personal injury damage in the first place eligible for compensation?", [["The WA insurance of Ruben's car.", "The WAM insurance also covers passengers' personal injury; only the driver himself falls outside the WA cover."], ["Ruben's AVP.", "The AVP excludes damage caused with a motor vehicle."], ["The Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer).", "The Guarantee Fund is intended for damage caused by unknown or uninsured vehicles; this car is insured."], ["The WA insurance of the lorry.", "The lorry was stationary and did not cause the accident; the fault lies with Ruben."]], "1f.2"],
["Daan borrows his neighbour's car and causes a collision in which another car is damaged. Which insurance compensates the damage to the other car?", [["The WA insurance of the neighbour's car.", "WA cover belongs to the motor vehicle and also covers the liability of every driver who drives it with permission. The neighbour may, however, drop on the bonus-malus ladder."], ["Daan's AVP, because he is not the registered keeper.", "Liability involving a motor vehicle is excluded in an AVP; it belongs under the WA insurance of the vehicle."], ["The WA insurance of any car of Daan's own, which always takes precedence.", "The insurance of Daan's own car relates to that vehicle; damage with the borrowed car falls under the WA of that car."], ["Nobody, because a driver who is not named never falls under the WA insurance.", "The WAM protects injured parties regardless of who is driving; the insurance also covers the liability of occasional drivers."]], "1f.2"],
["Daan has no car of his own and borrows his neighbour Gerrit's car for a house move. While parking he hits a wall, damaging the bumper and a headlight of Gerrit's car. The car has only a WA insurance. Daan does have an AVP. What applies to the damage to Gerrit's car?", [["It is not covered: WA does not cover damage to the car itself and the AVP excludes motor vehicles.", "The WA insurance covers damage to others, not to the insured vehicle; the AVP excludes damage with and to motor vehicles."], ["Gerrit's WA insurance covers the damage, because Daan is a different person from Gerrit.", "WA covers the liability of every driver towards third parties, but never the damage to the insured vehicle itself."], ["Daan's AVP covers the damage as damage to a borrowed item.", "The AVP excludes damage to and with motor vehicles; moreover, damage to items in custody is often covered only to a limited extent."], ["The damage is compensated by the Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer).", "The Guarantee Fund does not compensate damage to the insured vehicle itself."]], "1c.6"],
["Henk and Ria take out the car insurance for their new family car in Henk's name. They give Henk as the regular driver, because that results in a lower premium. In reality their 19-year-old son Kevin drives the car to work almost daily. After a claim with Kevin behind the wheel, this comes out. How should this be assessed?", [["As an incorrect statement about the regular driver, with possible consequences for the payment.", "The regular driver is a fact relevant to premium and acceptance; because the statement was deliberate, the insurer can assume intent to mislead and refuse payment."], ["There is no problem, as long as Henk is the policyholder and the owner of the car.", "It is not the owner but the actual regular driver that is decisive for the risk and the premium."], ["Only a premium matter: the insurer charges the difference afterwards, and the payment remains in full.", "An incorrect statement can go further than a premium correction; in the case of intent to mislead, the right to payment lapses."], ["The counterparty's damage is not compensated, because Kevin was not named as a driver.", "Under the WAM the injured counterparty is compensated; the insurer can, however, recover the amount from its insured."]], "1a.8"],
["What is the green card, the international certificate of insurance for motor vehicles, used for?", [["As proof of a valid WA insurance in the countries of the green card system.", "The green card shows that the insurance obligation in the country visited has been met; the card states in which countries the cover applies."], ["As proof that the vehicle is also insured abroad against theft and own damage.", "The green card concerns the compulsory liability insurance, not casco cover."], ["As a replacement for the registration certificate when the driver drives in a country outside the European Union.", "The registration certificate is still needed; the green card is a certificate of insurance and not a registration document."], ["As proof that the driver holds an international driving licence for the countries on the card.", "A driving licence concerns the driver's entitlement; the green card concerns the insurance of the vehicle."]], "1f.2"],
["Mr Hendriks puts his classic car in the garage for a year and has the registration number suspended with the Netherlands Vehicle Authority (RDW). What does this mean for the insurance obligation?", [["No insurance obligation then applies, but the vehicle may not go on the public road.", "A suspended vehicle may not take part in traffic or be parked on the public road; the obligation to have a WA insurance therefore lapses for that period."], ["The WAM insurance obligation remains, because it applies as long as a registered keeper is on record.", "Precisely because of the suspension, the insurance obligation lapses temporarily; the vehicle may then not go on the public road."], ["He may drive on the public road during the suspension, provided he has taken out an AVP.", "Driving with a suspended vehicle is not permitted, and an AVP does not cover liability involving motor vehicles."], ["During the suspension the insurance obligation passes to the RDW, which bears the vehicle's risk.", "The RDW registers the suspension but takes over no risk whatsoever; the vehicle simply may not take part in traffic."]], "1f.2"],
["Mrs Smits is driving in Germany during her holiday. At a roundabout she is hit by a German motorist, who admits fault. Her car is badly damaged. Back in the Netherlands she wants to recover her damage, but she does not speak German. What is the easiest way for her to settle her damage?", [["Through the claims representative that the German WA insurer must have appointed in the Netherlands.", "Under the European rules for motor vehicle insurance, every WA insurer has a claims representative in each EU country; she can therefore claim in the Netherlands and in Dutch."], ["Only through proceedings before a German court at the place of the accident.", "Proceedings are not necessary; the European scheme makes settlement possible via a claims representative in the Netherlands."], ["Through the Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer), because the party who caused the damage lives abroad.", "The Guarantee Fund is for unknown or uninsured motor vehicles, not for a known, insured foreign party who caused the damage."], ["Through her own WA insurance, which compensates damage to her own car.", "Her WA insurance covers only damage she causes to others, not damage to her own car."]], "1h.22"],
["What is the main purpose of the European Accident Statement after a collision?", [["Jointly with the counterparty, to record the course of events and both parties' details unambiguously.", "The form records the facts directly after the accident, which speeds up settlement between insurers and limits disputes about the course of events."], ["To establish at the scene of the accident, in a binding way, which driver is liable and to what percentage.", "The form records facts; liability is assessed afterwards by the insurers. Signing is not an admission of fault."], ["To inform the police, so that an official report can be drawn up.", "The accident statement is intended for the insurers and does not replace a police report or official report."], ["To have the damage paid out immediately by the insurer of whoever fills in the form first.", "Who fills in the form says nothing about who is liable or which insurer pays."]], "1h.22"],
["When is a car an economic total loss after a claim?", [["When repair costs more than the pre-damage value minus the residual value.", "Repair is then economically not sensible: it costs more than the value recovered by repair. The insurer then compensates the difference between the value before the damage and the residual value."], ["When the car is so badly damaged that technical repair is no longer possible.", "That is a technical total loss; in an economic total loss repair is possible, but too expensive."], ["When the car is older than ten years, regardless of the extent of the damage.", "The age of the car does not determine whether there is a total loss; it concerns the ratio between repair costs and value."], ["When the repair costs are higher than the excess on the casco insurance.", "Damage above the excess is simply repaired and compensated; that has nothing to do with total loss."]], "1d.8"],
["What does a purchase-value scheme in a casco insurance for a new car entail?", [["In the event of total loss or theft shortly after purchase, the purchase value is reimbursed, not the current value.", "A new car quickly loses value. This scheme protects the owner against that in the first years, tapering off over time according to the policy."], ["The premium is set on the basis of the purchase price and remains the same throughout the term.", "The scheme concerns the compensation in the event of total loss or theft, not the level of the premium."], ["At every repair only new original parts are used, without deduction for wear.", "That is an agreement on repair; a purchase-value scheme concerns the compensation if the car is lost."], ["The insurer buys the car back at the end of the term at the original purchase value.", "An insurer does not buy back cars; the scheme only determines the compensation in the event of covered damage."]], "1d.8"],
["The Mulder family tows a caravan with their car to the south of France. On a mountain road Mr Mulder makes a steering error, so that the caravan swings out and hits an oncoming car. The caravan itself is also badly damaged. The car has a WA insurance; for the caravan they have arranged nothing separately. How is this insured?", [["The damage to the other car falls under the car's WA; the damage to the caravan itself does not.", "For liability, a coupled caravan falls under the WA of the towing motor vehicle; for damage to the caravan itself a caravan insurance is needed."], ["All the damage falls under the car's WA, so also the damage to the caravan.", "The WA insurance covers damage to others, not to one's own vehicle or one's own caravan."], ["The damage to the other car falls under Mr Mulder's AVP.", "The AVP excludes damage involving motor vehicles; a coupled caravan falls under the car's WA."], ["Nothing is covered, because the accident happened abroad.", "WA cover applies in the countries of the green card system, including France."]], "1c.6"],
["Ilse rides an ordinary electric bicycle with pedal assistance up to 25 km/h and causes damage to a parked car. Which insurance in principle covers her liability?", [["Her AVP, because such an e-bike legally counts as a bicycle.", "A bicycle with pedal assistance up to 25 km/h is not a motor vehicle within the meaning of the WAM. The liability therefore falls under the private liability insurance."], ["A compulsory WA insurance for the e-bike, because an electric motor is present.", "That obligation applies to the speed pedelec and other motor vehicles, not to an ordinary e-bike up to 25 km/h."], ["Her bicycle insurance, because besides theft it always covers the cyclist's liability.", "A bicycle insurance usually covers theft and damage to the bicycle itself, not liability towards others."], ["The WA insurance of the damaged car, because it belongs to the vehicle.", "The WA of the parked car covers the liability of that car's driver, not damage that someone else causes to the car."]], "1h.24"],
["Ahmed drives a twelve-year-old car with a current value of about €2,500. He has been paying premium for years for an all-risks insurance with an excess of €300. He drives little and can absorb an unexpected expense of a few thousand euros himself. At an annual check he asks whether his insurance is still appropriate. Which advice best fits the duty of care?", [["Discuss whether WA or WA with limited casco fits the value of the car better.", "With a low current value, the premium for full casco is often out of proportion to the maximum payment; Ahmed can bear this risk himself."], ["Leave the all-risks cover in place, because it always offers the best protection for any car.", "The broadest cover is not automatically appropriate; premium, current value and financial capacity must be weighed against each other."], ["Let the WA cover lapse and keep only the casco cover.", "WA is legally compulsory for a motor vehicle on the public road and cannot lapse."], ["Lower the excess to zero, so that he has everything reimbursed.", "A lower excess increases the premium and does not solve the problem of the low current value."]], "1c.7"],
["Why is the WOZ value not a good basis for the insured amount of a home insurance?", [["It is a market value including land, whereas what matters is the rebuilding cost of the building.", "Land cannot burn down, and market value says little about building costs. The insured amount is therefore based on the rebuilding value."], ["The WOZ value is set only once every five years and is therefore always far too low.", "The WOZ value is determined annually; the problem is not the frequency but the fact that it measures a different value."], ["The WOZ value may be used only by the municipality and is not available to insurers.", "WOZ values are in principle publicly viewable in the Netherlands; the reason is substantive, not availability."], ["The WOZ value already takes underinsurance into account and would therefore count twice in the policy.", "The WOZ value is a valuation for taxation and takes no account of insurance concepts such as underinsurance."]], "1d.4"],
["A customer takes out a mortgage for the purchase of a home. Why does the lender almost always require a buildings insurance?", [["Because the home is the collateral and its value must be protected in the event of damage.", "If the home is lost without insurance, the lender loses its security. It therefore makes a home insurance a condition."], ["Because the buildings insurance repays the mortgage debt on the customer's death.", "Repayment on death is the function of a term life insurance; a buildings insurance covers damage to the building."], ["Because a buildings insurance is legally compulsory for every owner of a home in the Netherlands.", "There is no statutory obligation; the requirement stems from the lender's conditions."], ["Because the lender can thus receive commission on an insurance that goes with the loan.", "The requirement serves the security of the collateral; any remuneration is not the reason for the condition."]], "1c.4"],
["A home in Groningen has cracks caused by earthquakes resulting from gas extraction. Where can the owner turn in the first place?", [["The Groningen Mining Damage Institute (Instituut Mijnbouwschade Groningen).", "Damage caused by mining in Groningen falls under a public scheme implemented by this institute. An ordinary home insurance policy usually excludes earthquake damage."], ["His own home insurer, because earthquake damage is covered as standard under all-risks cover.", "Earthquake is usually excluded in Dutch home insurance policies; mining damage is handled via a separate public route."], ["Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute), because this dispute about compensation concerns a financial undertaking.", "Kifid deals with complaints about financial service providers; the handling of mining damage is not placed there."], ["The Dutch Reinsurance Company for Terrorism Damage.", "The terrorism reinsurer reinsures terrorism damage and has no role in damage caused by gas extraction."]], "1h.6"],
["Mr Vos lives in a 1930s house in Rotterdam. Because of a groundwater level that has fallen for years, the wooden pile foundation has been affected and has subsided. As a result there are cracks in the facades and the doors jam. He reports the damage to his buildings insurer. Is this usually covered?", [["No, the damage arose gradually and not through a sudden event.", "Home insurance policies cover sudden and unforeseen damage; foundation damage caused by gradual processes is excluded in most policies."], ["Yes, because cracks in walls and facades always fall under all-risks cover.", "All-risks also covers only sudden and unforeseen damage; gradual subsidence falls outside it."], ["Yes, but only if he engages an expert of his own.", "An expert establishes cause and extent, but does not make excluded damage covered."], ["Yes, because the cause is beyond his own control.", "The fact that the cause is beyond his control does not make gradually arisen damage covered."]], "1h.2"],
["Which water damage is usually NOT covered in a home or home contents policy?", [["Damage caused by water entering the cellar through a rising groundwater level.", "Groundwater rises gradually and is a general area risk; policies usually exclude this damage."], ["Damage caused by water suddenly flowing from a burst water pipe in the bathroom.", "Water suddenly flowing from a pipe due to a defect is a classic covered event."], ["Damage caused by rain entering through the damaged roof after storm damage.", "This is consequential damage of a covered storm, and as a rule it is also reimbursed."], ["Damage caused by water from a washing machine whose supply hose suddenly comes loose.", "Water suddenly released from a connected appliance usually falls under the policy's cover."]], "1h.2"],
["The Kuipers family has sixteen solar panels permanently mounted on the roof of their owner-occupied home, for well over €7,000. Their buildings insurance was taken out three years ago with an insured amount based on a rebuilding-value calculator. They assume that the panels are automatically co-insured. What is the main point of attention for the insurance?", [["Check whether the panels are co-insured with the home and whether the insured amount is still sufficient.", "Permanently mounted panels belong to the building in most policies, but conditions or maxima sometimes apply; the increase in value may make the insured amount insufficient."], ["Take out a separate home contents insurance for the panels, because they are movable.", "Permanently mounted panels belong to the building in most policies, not to the contents."], ["Do nothing, because indexation automatically absorbs the increase in value of the home.", "Indexation follows building costs, not an extension of the home with new components."], ["Have the panels included in the AVP, because they can cause damage to others.", "The AVP covers liability towards others, not damage to the panels themselves."]], "1c.3"],
["Which of the following possessions is usually NOT covered by a home contents insurance?", [["The family's passenger car.", "Motor vehicles are excluded from the concept of home contents; they are insured through a car insurance with WA and possibly casco."], ["The furniture and floor covering in the living room.", "Loose furniture and floor covering are classic contents of a private household."], ["The clothes and shoes of the family members.", "Clothing belongs to movable property for private use and therefore falls under the contents."], ["A laptop that the family uses at home for private purposes.", "Audiovisual and computer equipment for private use falls under the contents, often with its own maximum."]], "1c.5"],
["A home contents policy compensates on a replacement-value basis. For which items does compensation on a current-value basis nevertheless apply in many policies?", [["For items with a current value below a fixed share of the replacement value, often 40%.", "For heavily aged items, replacement value would give too much enrichment. Policies therefore limit the compensation to current value below a fixed threshold."], ["For all items bought less than a year ago.", "For recently bought items current value and replacement value are close together; replacement-value compensation is least objectionable there."], ["For items lost in a fire, because fire is a major risk.", "The cause of the damage does not determine whether compensation is on current value or replacement value; it concerns the condition of the item."], ["For items the insured paid for in cash instead of through the bank.", "The way of payment at purchase has no influence on the basis of compensation."]], "1d.4"],
["After a burglary it turns out that the back door was not fitted with the lock that the home contents policy prescribes. What can be the consequence?", [["The insurer can limit or refuse the cover for theft, depending on what the policy provides.", "Policies often set requirements for burglary prevention. If these are not met, the theft cover may lapse or be limited under the conditions."], ["No consequence, because requirements for locks apply only to business premises and not to private homes.", "Private home contents policies also regularly set requirements for locks or a quality mark for burglary prevention."], ["The insurer must pay in full, but may double the premium for the following year.", "The policy conditions determine the consequence for the cover; a fixed premium doubling as a sanction is not the rule."], ["The insurer may terminate all the customer's other policies immediately with retroactive effect.", "Failing to meet a prevention requirement is not fraud; it does not justify termination of other policies with retroactive effect."]], "1b.2"],
["Karim and Lisa buy an existing home in Amersfoort. The purchase agreement is signed in March and the transfer at the notary is on 1 June. The deed of sale states that the risk passes at transfer. The seller cancels his buildings insurance as of 1 June. From when must Karim and Lisa in any case insure the home themselves?", [["From the transfer at the notary on 1 June.", "With the transfer, ownership and, according to the deed, the risk pass; from that moment they have the interest and the lender also requires a buildings insurance."], ["From the signing of the purchase agreement in March.", "According to this deed the risk does not yet pass at signing; the seller bears the risk until the transfer."], ["From the day they actually move into the home.", "Occupation is not decisive; the moment the risk passes follows from the deed: the transfer."], ["Only after the seller's notice period has expired.", "The seller's notice period is not decisive for their own insurable interest."]], "1c.3"],
["Sem is 17 and lives with his parents. He skateboards on the street, loses control and makes a deep scratch in the neighbour's car. The neighbour sends the bill to Sem's parents. The family has a family AVP on which Sem is co-insured. Who is in principle liable?", [["Sem himself; the family's AVP can cover his liability.", "From the age of 16 a child is liable himself; the special parental liability no longer applies. As a co-insured, Sem has cover under the family AVP."], ["The parents, because Sem is still a minor and lives with them.", "Being a minor alone is not enough; the special parental liability applies until the age of 16."], ["The parents, unless they can prove that they cannot be blamed.", "This rule applies to children aged 14 and 15; Sem is 17."], ["Nobody, because it concerns an accident while playing.", "An accident can also be a wrongful act; Sem acted carelessly."]], "1h.28"],
["Mrs Dekker lives in her own home with an old tiled roof. The roof has not been maintained for years and several tiles are loose. In moderate wind a roof tile falls on the parked car of a visitor of the neighbours. The visitor holds Mrs Dekker liable. Which liability is at issue here?", [["Strict liability of the possessor of a defective structure, even without fault.", "The possessor of a structure that does not meet the requirements that may be set for it is liable for the damage arising from it; fault is not required."], ["Fault-based liability, where the visitor has to prove her negligence.", "Strict liability applies to defective structures; the injured party does not have to prove fault."], ["No liability, because the wind blew the tile off the roof.", "In moderate wind there is no question of force majeure; the cause is the defect in the roof."], ["Liability of the neighbours, because their visitor was parked there.", "The neighbours have nothing to do with the roof tile; the possessor of the home is liable."]], "1c.11"],
["Jesse (15) lives with his father. Both are insured on the same family AVP. While gaming, Jesse knocks a glass of cola over his father's laptop. The laptop is irreparably damaged and his father wants to claim the damage under the AVP. What usually applies?", [["No cover, because property damage between insureds on the same policy is excluded.", "In most AVP policies property damage of co-insureds is excluded, to prevent claims within the family."], ["Full cover, because Jesse is liable for the damage to the laptop.", "Even if there is liability, the policy excludes property damage between co-insureds."], ["Cover, but only for the current value of the laptop.", "There is no cover; the valuation basis therefore plays no role."], ["Cover, because the AVP treats damage within the family the same as damage to others.", "Precisely not: property damage between co-insureds is excluded in most policies."]], "1h.2"],
["An insured, as a cyclist, causes an accident in which a pedestrian is injured. The pedestrian also claims compensation for pain and suffering. Does that fall under the AVP?", [["Yes, in the case of liability for injury the AVP also covers non-material damage such as compensation for pain and suffering.", "Personal injury damage comprises both material damage, such as loss of income, and non-material damage. Both fall within the liability cover."], ["No, an AVP compensates only material damage, such as medical costs and damage to clothing.", "Non-material damage for which the insured is liable also falls under the cover for personal injury damage."], ["No, compensation for pain and suffering is always paid by the victim's health insurer.", "A health insurer reimburses healthcare costs, not compensation for pain and suffering; that must be paid by the liable party."], ["Only if the court has fixed the compensation for pain and suffering, and then up to half of the amount.", "Compensation for pain and suffering can also be fixed in a settlement, and the AVP covers it in full within the sum insured."]], "1h.26"],
["What does loss-of-affection damage (affectieschade) entail in Dutch liability law?", [["Compensation for the grief of close relatives in the case of serious permanent injury to, or the death of, a loved one.", "Since the introduction of the law on affection damage, certain close relatives can themselves receive compensation for their grief; the liable party and its insurer pay."], ["Compensation for the emotional value of a damaged item, such as a family heirloom.", "Affection damage concerns the grief of close relatives in the case of serious injury or death, not attachment to an object."], ["Compensation that the victim himself receives for pain and sorrow after an accident.", "That is compensation for pain and suffering for the victim himself; affection damage is a compensation of their own for the close relatives."], ["Damage that arises because someone receives a shock on witnessing an accident involving a stranger.", "That resembles shock damage, to which different requirements apply; affection damage is linked to a close bond with the victim."]], "1h.26"],
["A pedestrian crosses unexpectedly without looking and is hit by a cyclist who was also riding too fast. What does the pedestrian's fault mean for his compensation?", [["His compensation can be reduced in proportion to his own fault.", "If the injured party himself contributed to the damage, the compensation is divided according to the extent to which each one's conduct contributed to the damage, possibly adjusted on grounds of reasonableness."], ["No consequence, because a pedestrian is always compensated in full in a collision.", "The special protection of pedestrians applies against motor vehicles; against a cyclist the ordinary contributory-fault rule applies."], ["He receives no compensation, because he himself acted in breach of the traffic rules.", "Contributory fault leads to a division of the damage, not automatically to loss of any compensation."], ["The cyclist and the pedestrian each always bear exactly half of all the damage.", "There is no fixed division of half; the division depends on each one's concrete contribution to the accident."]], "1h.26"],
["When is conduct unlawful under the Dutch Civil Code?", [["In the case of infringement of a right, breach of a statutory duty or breach of unwritten social standards of care.", "These are the three categories of unlawfulness. In addition, attributability, damage and a causal connection, among other things, must be satisfied for liability."], ["Only in the case of breach of a statutory provision that carries a penalty, such as a provision of the Criminal Code.", "Unlawfulness is much broader than punishable conduct; careless conduct can also be unlawful."], ["Only if the offender intended to cause damage to the injured party or consciously accepted it.", "Intent is not a requirement; carelessness can also constitute a wrongful act."], ["Only if there was a contract between the offender and the injured party that the offender breached.", "Breach of contract is non-performance; a wrongful act in fact presupposes no contract between the parties."]], "1f.6"],
["A young couple with a limited budget wants to choose which personal insurance to take out first. Why is the AVP often seen as one of the most important?", [["Because the damage for which one can be liable, such as personal injury, can run up to an unlimited amount.", "A liability claim can disrupt someone's financial position for years. Insuring fits precisely a small chance of a very large loss, and the premium is relatively low."], ["Because an AVP is legally compulsory for everyone who lives in the Netherlands.", "An AVP is not legally compulsory; only the WA insurance for motor vehicles carries a statutory obligation."], ["Because the AVP also compensates damage to one's own belongings, so that a home contents insurance becomes unnecessary.", "An AVP covers damage to others, not to one's own property; a home contents insurance remains necessary for that."], ["Because an AVP pays out most often of all personal insurances.", "The AVP does not pay out most often; its value lies precisely in protection against rare but very large claims."]], "1c.4"],
["Which damage is as a rule NOT covered in an AVP?", [["Pure financial loss that does not result from damage to a person or item.", "An AVP covers liability for damage to persons and items, with the consequential damage thereof. A purely financial disadvantage without injury or property damage usually falls outside the cover."], ["The lost income of someone who was injured by the insured.", "Loss of income due to injury is consequential damage of personal injury and therefore does fall under the cover."], ["The cost of repairing a bicycle that the insured accidentally knocked over.", "This is property damage to a third party and falls within the cover, unless the bicycle was in the insured's custody."], ["The medical costs of a passer-by bitten by the insured's dog.", "Damage to a third party caused by one's own pet is a usual covered liability within the AVP."]], "1h.2"],
["What is the difference between a legal expenses insurance in kind and a cost-reimbursement insurance?", [["In kind, the insurer helps itself with its own lawyers; with a cost-reimbursement insurance it reimburses an external adviser.", "In kind, the help itself is the performance. With a cost-reimbursement insurance the insured chooses an adviser and the insurer reimburses the adviser's costs within the policy."], ["In kind, the insured receives a fixed amount; with a cost-reimbursement insurance all costs are reimbursed without limit.", "In kind does not mean a fixed amount but help in the form of services; and a cost-reimbursement insurance also has maxima."], ["In kind there is no waiting period; with a cost-reimbursement insurance a waiting period of one year always applies.", "Waiting periods depend on the policy and the type of dispute, not on the form in which the help is provided."], ["There is no difference; insurers use both terms interchangeably for the same product.", "They are different forms of cover, each with different consequences for who actually provides the help."]], "1d.15"],
["Why do legal expenses insurances often apply a minimum interest before they provide help?", [["To prevent the costs of help from being out of proportion to the interest of the dispute.", "In a dispute over a small amount, the costs of legal help are often higher than the interest. A threshold keeps the premium affordable."], ["To ensure that only disputes with a government body fall under the cover.", "A minimum interest says something about the size of the dispute, not about the counterparty."], ["To force the insured always to submit small disputes to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute).", "Kifid deals only with complaints about financial service providers, not with every small dispute of an insured."], ["Because the law provides that disputes below a certain amount cannot be brought before the court.", "Small disputes can also be brought before the subdistrict court; the threshold is an agreement in the policy."]], "1d.3"],
["An insured wants to choose a lawyer himself in proceedings before the subdistrict court, where a lawyer is not compulsory. The legal expenses insurer wants to have the case handled by its own lawyer. What applies according to European case law?", [["Free choice applies in every procedure, even if a lawyer is not compulsory.", "The Court of Justice of the EU has ruled that free choice of lawyer may not be limited to proceedings with compulsory legal representation. A policy may, however, set a reasonable maximum for external costs."], ["Free choice applies only in proceedings in which a lawyer is legally compulsory.", "That limitation was in fact rejected by the Court of Justice; free choice also exists before the subdistrict court."], ["The insurer may always decide itself who handles the case, because it pays the costs.", "The right to free choice protects the insured against an insurer that unilaterally decides who handles the case."], ["The insured may choose only if he himself pays the costs of the chosen lawyer.", "With free choice the insurer reimburses the costs within the policy conditions; the choice does not depend on paying oneself."]], "1h.29"],
["Why do many insurers that also offer other non-life insurances have legal expenses handled by a separate entity, such as a foundation?", [["To prevent conflicts of interest, for example in a dispute with another customer of that insurer.", "European rules require that the handling of legal expenses cases be separated from other classes or that free choice of legal representative be offered. A separate handler is a common solution."], ["To make the legal expenses premium tax-deductible for the insured.", "The legal form of the handler has no influence on the tax treatment of the premium for a private individual."], ["Because lawyers may be employed only by a foundation and not by an insurer.", "There is no rule that lawyers may work only for foundations; it is about separating interests."], ["Because under professional rules lawyers may be employed only by a foundation and not by an insurer.", "A consumer can also complain to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) about legal expenses; the separate handler serves independence."]], "1a.12"],
["A car insurance contains cover for recovery legal expenses. What does that entail?", [["Help in recovering one's own damage from the liable counterparty.", "Recovery legal expenses help the insured to claim his damage from whoever is liable. It is not a defence against claims from others."], ["Help in defending against a claim from a counterparty who holds the insured liable.", "Defence against a claim falls under the WA cover, where the insurer conducts the defence; recovery assistance works the other way round."], ["Legal help in all disputes of the insured, including outside traffic.", "Recovery legal expenses under a car policy are limited to recovering damage from traffic accidents with the insured vehicle."], ["Reimbursement of fines that the insured must pay after a traffic offence.", "Fines are never insurable; recovery legal expenses concern the recovery of damage."]], "1d.15"],
["Mr Yilmaz gets into a conflict with his employer about his dismissal. Which part of a modular legal expenses insurance is needed for this?", [["The work and income module.", "Employment conflicts fall under the module that covers work, dismissal and income. Without that module there is no cover for this dispute."], ["The consumer and housing module, because it concerns a dispute of a private individual.", "The consumer and housing module covers disputes about purchases and the home, not the employment relationship."], ["The traffic module, because Mr Yilmaz travels to work by car.", "The way of travelling to work does not turn a dismissal conflict into a traffic matter."], ["No module, because employment conflicts can be handled only through a trade union.", "A trade union can help, but employment conflicts are indeed insurable through legal expenses insurance."]], "1c.4"],
["An insured is suspected of an intentional offence. How do many legal expenses policies deal with this?", [["No cover in advance, but after an acquittal the costs are reimbursed after all.", "Insurers do not want to provide help for intentionally punishable conduct. If the suspicion turns out to be unfounded, the costs are reimbursed after an acquittal after all."], ["There is always full cover, because every suspect has a right to a lawyer.", "The right to a lawyer does not mean that a legal expenses insurer bears the costs for every intentional offence."], ["There is cover only if the insured confesses guilt, so that the case can be dealt with quickly.", "Making cover dependent on a confession would be contrary to the rights of a suspect and is not a customary arrangement."], ["The insurer pays any fine, but not the lawyer's costs.", "Fines are not insurable; the arrangement in fact concerns the costs of legal assistance."]], "1d.3"],
["Jasper (24) has a continuous travel insurance. After his studies he wants to take an eight-month world trip through South America and Asia. He assumes he is well insured with his continuous policy. He asks his adviser for confirmation. What is the main point of attention?", [["A maximum uninterrupted travel duration usually applies; for this trip a separate policy may be needed.", "Continuous travel insurances in most policies cover trips up to a maximum duration of a few months; for longer trips a separate insurance is needed."], ["A continuous travel insurance covers trips of any duration, as long as the premium is paid.", "Continuous policies in most cases have a maximum travel duration per trip."], ["He only needs to check his health insurance, because it reimburses all costs abroad.", "The health insurance often reimburses care abroad only up to the Dutch rate and does not cover luggage and repatriation."], ["He must cancel his continuous policy, because during the trip he does not live in the Netherlands.", "A temporary trip is not emigration; the point of attention is the policy's maximum travel duration."]], "1d.14"],
["What does the European Health Insurance Card (EHIC) offer a Dutch insured during a temporary stay in another EU country?", [["Necessary care under the same conditions as for residents of that country.", "The card entitles the holder to necessary care at local conditions and rates. Costs that are for one's own account there or are higher are not covered; a travel insurance serves that purpose."], ["Full reimbursement of all medical costs, including repatriation to the Netherlands.", "Repatriation does not fall under the card; a travel insurance with assistance is needed for that."], ["Cover for theft of luggage and cancellation of the trip.", "The card concerns medical care only, not luggage or cancellation."], ["A replacement for the health insurance, so that it can be stopped during the trip.", "The card in fact works on the basis of the Dutch health insurance; it must remain in force."]], "1h.31"],
["During a holiday in Spain, Mark falls and breaks his thigh bone. He is operated on in a Spanish hospital and cannot sit yet. The doctors advise transport lying down with medical supervision to the Netherlands. Mark has a travel insurance with assistance. What role does the travel insurer's emergency assistance centre play?", [["It arranges the help and the medical transport home, in consultation with the treating doctors.", "The emergency assistance centre coordinates medical assistance and repatriation and assesses together with doctors whether and how transport is advisable."], ["It reimburses only the hospital costs; Mark must arrange the transport himself.", "Repatriation and transport are precisely a core task of the emergency assistance centre in a travel insurance with assistance."], ["It takes action only after Mark has advanced the costs and claimed them.", "The emergency assistance centre must in fact be engaged beforehand, so that it can arrange the help and guarantee payment."], ["It refers Mark to his health insurer, which arranges the transport to the Netherlands.", "The basic insurance does not reimburse repatriation; the travel insurance arranges that."]], "1d.14"],
["Emma and her friends are going on a winter sports holiday to Austria in February. Emma also wants to ski off-piste there, in deep snow outside the marked pistes. She has a continuous travel insurance without additional modules. She asks her adviser whether she is well insured like this. What should the adviser look out for?", [["Whether winter sports are co-insured and whether skiing off-piste is not excluded.", "In most policies winter sports are a separate module and off-piste skiing is excluded or covered only under conditions."], ["Nothing, because skiing in Europe always falls under the basic cover.", "In most policies winter sports are an additional cover; the basic cover is then not sufficient."], ["Only whether she has a European Health Insurance Card, because that also covers search and rescue.", "The card entitles the holder to necessary care, but does not cover search, rescue or repatriation."], ["Whether her AVP also applies in Austria, because it covers her own injury.", "The AVP covers liability towards others, not Emma's own injury."]], "1d.14"],
["Niels takes a city trip to Barcelona by car. On the last day he leaves his €1,200 camera in the boot, while the car stands unattended on the street all day. On his return the car turns out to have been broken into and the camera has been stolen. He files a police report and reports the damage to his travel insurer. What is a usual point of discussion?", [["Many policies exclude or limit theft of valuables from unattended cars.", "In most travel policies valuables from a car left unattended are not covered or covered only to a limited extent, even in the case of forced entry."], ["Theft from a car is covered only if there are no signs of forced entry.", "Signs of forced entry in fact support the claim; the discussion concerns leaving it unattended."], ["A camera never counts as luggage, because it is an electronic device.", "Cameras do count as luggage in most policies, often with a separate maximum."], ["The damage must be claimed under the car's casco insurance.", "A casco insurance covers the car itself, not the items lying in it."]], "1h.2"],
["A travel insurance contains an accident section with a fixed amount on death or permanent disability. What kind of cover is this?", [["A fixed-sum insurance: a predetermined amount is paid.", "With accident cover the payment is a fixed amount or a percentage of it in the case of disability. The indemnity principle plays no role here."], ["A non-life insurance: the actual loss of income after the accident is compensated.", "The section pays fixed amounts and is therefore not a non-life insurance within the meaning of Book 7 of the Dutch Civil Code (BW)."], ["A liability insurance: the payment goes to the person who caused the accident.", "The accident section pays to the insured or his surviving relatives, not to a party who caused the accident."], ["A health insurance: the payment is intended for medical costs abroad.", "Medical costs fall under a separate section; the accident section pays a fixed amount."]], "1a.2"],
["A tour operator goes bankrupt just before a family is due to leave on a package holiday. Which scheme can protect the family if the organisation is affiliated?", [["The Travel Guarantee Fund Foundation (Stichting Garantiefonds Reisgelden), in the event of the tour operator's insolvency.", "With a package holiday the traveller is protected against the organiser's insolvency. For affiliated organisations the Guarantee Fund arranges repayment or continuation of the trip."], ["The Travel Calamities Fund (Calamiteitenfonds Reizen), which offers compensation if a tour operator goes bankrupt.", "The Calamities Fund concerns calamities at the destination, such as natural disasters, not the organisation's bankruptcy."], ["The family's cancellation insurance, which always covers bankruptcy of the tour operator.", "Cancellation insurances cover personal reasons for cancellation; bankruptcy of the organiser is usually not part of that."], ["The Motor Vehicles Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer), if the trip were to take place by coach.", "The Guarantee Fund concerns traffic damage caused by uninsured or unknown vehicles, not the bankruptcy of a tour operator."]], "1d.14"],
["What is meant by abandonment in an insurance for pleasure craft?", [["Surrendering the vessel to the insurer against payment of the insured amount.", "In abandonment the insured transfers his rights to the vessel or the wreck to the insurer, which in return pays the insured amount."], ["Leaving the vessel unattended in a harbour or marina.", "That is not abandonment in the insurance sense, although policies may attach conditions to it."], ["Damage to the vessel from a collision with another vessel under way.", "That is a collision; abandonment concerns surrendering the vessel to the insurer."], ["A deduction from the compensation because old parts are replaced by new ones.", "That is the 'new for old' deduction; abandonment is something different."]], "1d.12"],
["Why is osmosis damage to a polyester hull excluded in most casco policies for pleasure craft?", [["Because it is a gradual process that results from the nature of the material.", "Osmosis develops slowly as water penetrates the polyester; such gradual damage through inherent defect is excluded in most policies."], ["Because osmosis arises only when sailing outside the cruising area insured in the policy.", "Osmosis has nothing to do with the cruising area; it arises from prolonged contact of the polyester with water."], ["Because osmosis already falls under the compulsory WA insurance for pleasure craft.", "A WA insurance covers damage to others, not to one's own hull; moreover WA for pleasure craft is not legally compulsory in the Netherlands."], ["Because osmosis occurs only on boats older than fifty years.", "Osmosis can also occur on younger polyester boats; the exclusion follows from the gradual nature."]], "1c.10"],
["Jeroen (42) has a personal accident insurance. While moving a heavy cupboard he feels a sharp pain in his back; it later turns out he has a herniated disc. A month later he slips on a wet staircase and breaks his ankle, leaving him with permanent injury. He reports both events to his insurer. Which event most clearly meets the concept of accident in most accident policies?", [["The fall on the staircase: sudden violence coming from outside, with medically determinable injury.", "In most policies an accident is a sudden, involuntary effect of external violence on the body with medically determinable injury; the fall meets that."], ["The herniated disc, because it arose suddenly while lifting the cupboard.", "A herniated disc as a rule has an internal cause; in most policies it is not an accident or is expressly excluded."], ["Both events, because both led to injury.", "Injury alone is not enough; there must be sudden violence coming from outside on the body."], ["Neither, because they took place in and around his own home.", "A personal accident insurance in most policies applies around the clock, so also at home."]], "1d.20"],
["What cover sections does a personal accident insurance consist of in most policies?", [["Payment on death and on permanent disability, often supplemented with medical costs.", "These are the usual sections; for permanent disability a percentage of the insured amount is paid according to the degree of disability."], ["Compensation for loss of income until the AOW (state pension) age, with premium waiver.", "Those are features of a disability insurance, not of an accident insurance."], ["Compensation for damage to the property of others and legal help in a dispute.", "That belongs to a liability insurance or a legal expenses insurance."], ["Only a payment on death caused by a traffic accident.", "An accident insurance is broader: it also applies outside traffic and also covers permanent disability."]], "1c.18"],
["For which type of damage do home contents and travel policies usually set as a condition that the insured files a police report?", [["In the case of theft, burglary, robbery and vandalism.", "In the case of criminal offences insurers ask for a police report as confirmation of the event and to combat fraud."], ["In the case of any water damage from a burst pipe.", "A burst pipe is not a criminal offence; a report to the insurer is sufficient there."], ["In the case of storm and hail damage to the roof of the home.", "Natural events are not criminal offences; a police report is not at issue there."], ["In the case of damage that the insured accidentally caused to his own belongings.", "In the case of one's own carelessness there is no offender and no criminal offence, so no police report either."]], "1h.11"],
["What does it mean if a loss adjuster is registered with the NIVRE (Netherlands Institute of Registered Experts)?", [["That he is a member of the experts' professional organisation, with quality requirements and disciplinary law.", "Registration stands for quality and independence according to the rules of the professional organisation. In the event of a complaint about the conduct of a registered expert, insureds can also invoke disciplinary law."], ["That he may decide on behalf of the insurer, in a binding way, whether a claim is covered by the policy.", "An expert establishes the cause and extent of the damage; the insurer decides the question of cover."], ["That he holds a licence from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) to advise on insurances.", "An AFM licence concerns financial services; NIVRE registration concerns the professional group of experts."], ["That he may handle only claims below a certain amount.", "Registration says nothing about a maximum claim amount; experts handle claims of any size."]], "1h.6"],
["The insurer's expert has established the extent of a fire damage. Does this mean that the insurer must also compensate the damage?", [["No, the expert establishes cause and extent; the insurer assesses cover separately.", "Expert assessment and cover are different questions. The expert assessment is often carried out subject to cover."], ["Yes, as soon as an expert has established the damage, the insurer has acknowledged cover.", "Engaging an expert is not an acknowledgement of cover; the insurer can still invoke an exclusion."], ["Yes, unless the insured engages a counter-expert himself.", "Engaging a counter-expert concerns the extent of the damage, not the existence of cover."], ["No, the insured must first have the report approved by Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute).", "Kifid does not approve expert reports; it deals with complaints only after a dispute with the insurer."]], "1h.6"],
["An insurer discovers that an insured deliberately falsified invoices in a claim in order to obtain a higher payment. Which measures are usual?", [["Refuse payment, end the policy, recover costs and register the fraudster.", "In the case of fraud the right to payment lapses. Insurers can end the relationship, recover investigation costs, file a report and register the fraudster under the industry-wide protocol."], ["Refuse only the fraudulent part of the claim and pay out the rest in full.", "In the case of fraud the right to the whole payment for that event usually lapses, not just the exaggerated part."], ["Increase the premium for the coming year, but simply pay out the claim.", "A premium increase is not a customary response to fraud and does not leave the payment in place."], ["Do nothing, because only the police may take measures in the case of forgery.", "An insurer may act under civil law and under the industry protocol, in addition to any criminal case."]], "1h.13"],
["What is the purpose of the Code of Conduct for Handling Personal Injury Claims (Gedragscode Behandeling Letselschade)?", [["Careful and prompt settlement, with the victim at the centre.", "This self-regulation code sets standards for dealings between victim, representative and insurer, such as timely communication and respectful treatment."], ["Laying down fixed amounts of compensation for pain and suffering per type of injury.", "The code concerns the settlement process; compensation for pain and suffering is determined case by case, with case law as a guide."], ["Obliging victims to work only with a lawyer of the insurer.", "The code in fact recognises the victim's right to his own representative."], ["Regulating which insurer must pay the personal injury damage in the case of concurrence.", "Concurrence is regulated in the law and in agreements between insurers; the code concerns treatment and settlement."]], "1a.11"],
["Mrs Van Dijk's washing machine started leaking during the night. The floor covering in the living room is soaked and smells musty. She has reported the damage and the insurer will send an expert in three days. She wants to throw away the floor covering immediately to prevent mould. What is sensible advice?", [["Take photos, keep a piece and first consult the insurer before throwing it away.", "The insured must cooperate in the damage assessment; with photos, evidence and consultation she avoids discussion about extent and cause."], ["Throw everything away immediately; the expert can also establish the damage without the floor covering.", "Without evidence the expert will find it hard to establish the extent, which may jeopardise the payment."], ["Touch nothing until the expert arrives, not even to limit further damage.", "She must in fact take reasonable measures to limit damage, such as drying and ventilating."], ["Withdraw the damage report and replace the floor covering herself.", "She would then give up her right to compensation for covered damage for no reason."]], "1h.4"],
["Within what period must a consumer submit his complaint to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) after the insurer has taken a final position on the complaint?", [["Within three months.", "The rules of Kifid's Disputes Committee state a period of three months after the financial service provider's final position."], ["Within two weeks.", "Two weeks is much shorter than the period in the rules; the consumer has three months."], ["Within five years, equal to the limitation period of a claim arising from a wrongful act.", "The limitation period under the Dutch Civil Code (BW) is something different from the filing period at Kifid."], ["No period applies, as long as the claim has not yet become time-barred.", "The rules do contain a filing period after the final position."]], "1h.9"],
["What does a consumer pay to submit a complaint about his insurer to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute)?", [["Nothing; handling of the complaint is free of charge for consumers.", "Kifid is financed by the affiliated financial service providers; consumers pay no contribution for submitting a complaint."], ["A fixed amount that depends on the size of the claim.", "A contribution that grows with the claim does not apply to consumers at Kifid."], ["The court fee that also applies at the subdistrict court.", "Kifid is a dispute body outside the court; court fees do not apply there."], ["The insurer's costs, if the consumer loses the case.", "A consumer does not have to reimburse the insurer's costs at Kifid if his complaint is rejected."]], "1h.9"],
["The policy conditions of a home contents insurance contain a provision that can reasonably be read in two ways. How is that provision interpreted for a consumer?", [["In the sense that is most favourable to the consumer.", "In case of doubt about the meaning of a term in a contract with a consumer, the interpretation most favourable to him applies. That encourages insurers to write clear conditions."], ["In the sense that the insurer intended when drafting, because it wrote the conditions.", "It is precisely the drafter who bears the risk of unclarity, not the consumer."], ["In the sense that the adviser explained to the customer during the advice meeting.", "What an adviser says may be relevant to his own duty of care, but does not determine the interpretation of the policy."], ["The provision is void, so that the subject is no longer covered at all.", "Unclarity leads to interpretation in the consumer's favour, not to nullity and loss of cover."]], "1h.13"],
["Linda (35) lives in Enschede and gets a job as an employee at a company in Germany. She remains living in the Netherlands and crosses the border daily to her work. She no longer works in the Netherlands. She asks her adviser what this means for her Dutch health insurance. What is the starting point?", [["In principle she falls under the German system and is no longer subject to the Zvw insurance obligation.", "Under the European coordination rules an employee is insured in the country where he works; as a cross-border worker she can nevertheless receive care in the Netherlands through a registration."], ["She remains subject to the compulsory insurance under the Zvw, because she continues to live in the Netherlands.", "When working in another EU country, the country of work is in principle decisive, not the country of residence."], ["She must insure herself compulsorily in both countries and therefore also pay double premium.", "The European rules in fact prevent double insurance; there is one competent country."], ["She can make do with a Dutch supplementary insurance for her healthcare costs.", "A supplementary insurance does not replace statutory insurance; she falls under the German system."]], "1d.17"],
["Must a consumer with a dispute about an insurance payment first go to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) before he may go to court?", [["No, Kifid is an alternative; the consumer can also go directly to court.", "Kifid offers a low-threshold and free route, but access to the court remains open. However, a binding procedure once chosen can restrict the route to the court."], ["Yes, court proceedings are possible only after a ruling by Kifid.", "There is no statutory obligation to go to Kifid first; the court is directly accessible."], ["Yes, but only if the dispute concerns an amount lower than the sum insured.", "The size of the amount does not make Kifid a compulsory first step."], ["No, but after proceedings in court a consumer can still go to Kifid for a second opinion.", "As a rule Kifid does not deal with cases on which the court has already ruled; it is not an appeal body above the court."]], "1h.9"],
["What is the External Referral Register (Extern Verwijzingsregister) that financial institutions use via Stichting CIS (the CIS Foundation)?", [["A register of persons with an incident, such as fraud, as a warning to institutions.", "In this way institutions can warn one another about persons who pose a risk. Registration takes place under a fixed protocol, with requirements for care and duration."], ["A register of all consumers who have ever reported a claim to an insurer.", "An ordinary claim report does not lead to inclusion in this register; it concerns incidents such as fraud."], ["A public register in which anyone can check whether a person has a valid insurance.", "The register is not public; only affiliated institutions can consult it."], ["A register of advisers who have lost their licence from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM).", "The AFM register contains information on licences; the External Referral Register concerns customers with an incident."]], "1a.10"],
["A consumer believes he is wrongly listed in a CIS registration. Whom should he turn to first to have the registration corrected?", [["The insurer that made the registration.", "The institution that supplied the data is responsible for its accuracy. The consumer can ask it for correction or removal."], ["The Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM), which amends insurers' registrations on behalf of consumers.", "The AFM does not amend individual registrations; the responsible institution must do that."], ["The district court, because a registration can be struck out only by a judge.", "A judge can ultimately rule, but the first step is a request to the institution itself."], ["His own adviser, who is authorised to change registrations at CIS.", "An adviser has no authority to change CIS registrations; the registering institution does that."]], "1a.10"],
["Fleur (31) does almost all her shopping and banking online. Her friend recently became a victim of identity fraud and had to spend a lot of time and money to put everything right. Fleur has a home contents insurance, an AVP and a legal expenses insurance. She asks her adviser whether a personal cyber insurance could be worthwhile for her. What is the best argument for such an insurance?", [["Help and costs in the case of identity fraud and online scams are often not covered, or only to a limited extent, under her current policies.", "A personal cyber insurance focuses on digital risks such as identity fraud, online purchase fraud and data recovery, which other policies often cover not at all or only to a limited extent."], ["A cyber insurance replaces her AVP, because liability nowadays arises mainly online.", "A cyber insurance does not replace the AVP; the AVP remains necessary for damage she causes to others."], ["A cyber insurance is legally compulsory for everyone who banks or shops online.", "There is no statutory obligation for private individuals to insure against cyber risks."], ["A cyber insurance also covers theft of her laptop and phone from her home.", "Theft of items from the home belongs to the home contents insurance, not to a cyber insurance."]], "1c.4"],
["In which document is it laid down in Dutch policies what is meant by terrorism and how the cover works?", [["In the joint Terrorism Cover Clause Sheet (Clausuleblad terrorismedekking).", "Insurers include this clause sheet in their policies. It contains the definitions and refers to the payment protocol of the terrorism reinsurer for settlement."], ["In the Financial Supervision Act (Wft), which contains a fixed statutory definition of terrorism.", "The Wft regulates supervision and conduct of financial undertakings; the definition of terrorism in policies is in the clause sheet."], ["In the general conditions of each insurer separately, with its own description per insurer.", "It is precisely to achieve uniformity that insurers use a joint clause sheet with the same definitions."], ["In the molest clause, which completely excludes terrorism as a form of molest.", "Terrorism is regulated separately and is in fact covered within limits via the terrorism reinsurer, independently of the molest exclusion."]], "1h.2"],
["After a major terrorist attack, the total damage is higher than the amount the terrorism reinsurer can pay out per year. What is the consequence for insureds?", [["They receive a percentage determined by the terrorism reinsurer, paid out via their own insurer.", "Because of the annual maximum, the available capacity is divided proportionally. The insured receives the payment from his own insurer."], ["They receive no compensation at all, because terrorism is completely excluded when the maximum is exceeded.", "When the maximum is exceeded, the available capacity is divided; insureds then receive part of their damage."], ["They can claim the shortfall directly from the terrorism reinsurer, which has a supplementary fund for this.", "The terrorism reinsurer has no direct relationship with insureds and has a maximum capacity."], ["Only the first insureds to report their damage are compensated in full until the maximum is reached.", "The division is proportional for all insureds, not in order of reporting."]], "1h.10"],
["Which events, in addition to terrorism, fall under the scheme of the Terrorism Cover Clause Sheet?", [["Malicious contamination and preventive government measures.", "The clause sheet also covers deliberate contamination with, for example, pathogens and measures that the government takes to prevent terrorism or limit its consequences."], ["Civil disturbance and riot, which fall entirely under the terrorism scheme.", "Civil disturbance and riot are forms of molest that fall outside the terrorism scheme."], ["Flooding caused by the failure of a primary flood defence.", "That is a flood risk to which other rules apply; it does not fall under the clause sheet."], ["All forms of vandalism by unknown offenders.", "Ordinary vandalism is a regularly insured or excluded risk and does not fall under the terrorism scheme."]], "1h.2"],
["What is meant by a primary flood defence?", [["A statutorily designated defence against water from the sea, large rivers or lakes.", "Primary flood defences protect against external water and are laid down in law. Damage caused by their failure is usually excluded in personal policies."], ["Any embankment along a ditch or canal in a polder, managed and maintained by the water board.", "Embankments along canals and ditches are regional or non-primary flood defences, not primary defences."], ["The gutter and drainage of a home that must drain rainwater.", "A gutter is not a flood defence; the concept concerns national protection against flooding."], ["A temporary sandbag wall put up by the fire brigade during high water.", "An emergency measure is not a statutorily designated flood defence."]], "1c.1"],
["When can an affected person claim compensation under the Disaster Damage Compensation Act (Wts)?", [["Only once the government has opened the scheme for that specific disaster.", "The Wts does not work automatically: for each disaster it is decided whether and for which area the scheme opens. After that the conditions of that scheme apply."], ["Always, as soon as the damage is greater than the excess on the home insurance.", "The Wts is not a supplement to every insured damage; it applies only if the government opens it for a disaster."], ["Only if the insurer has rejected the damage in writing because of an exclusion.", "A rejection by the insurer is not a condition; the government's decision to open the scheme is decisive."], ["Exclusively if the affected person is a member of a water board in the affected area.", "Membership of a water board is not a condition for compensation."]], "1c.1"],
["What is usually the difference in home and home contents policies between damage caused by precipitation and damage caused by flooding?", [["Precipitation is rain, snow or hail that penetrates; flooding is water that goes beyond banks or defences.", "Precipitation damage is often covered. In the case of flooding the cover depends on the cause and the type of flood defence, and failure of a primary defence is usually excluded."], ["There is no difference; in the policy both terms mean that water from outside enters the home and causes damage.", "Policies in fact make a clear distinction, because the cover for the two differs greatly."], ["Precipitation is water from a broken water pipe; flooding is water from the sewer.", "Water from a pipe is mains water, not precipitation; the concepts are being confused here."], ["Precipitation is covered only under all-risks policies; flooding only under policies based on named perils.", "The distinction concerns the cause of the water, not the type of cover."]], "1d.2"],
["Daniël plays saxophone as an amateur in a big band. He takes his €6,000 saxophone every week to rehearsals and performances across the country. The instrument is co-insured on his home contents insurance, which offers only limited cover outside the home. After a performance the case falls from his hands and the saxophone is damaged. Which insurance best fits his situation?", [["A separate instrument insurance that also covers damage outside the home.", "An instrument insurance is intended for instruments that often go along with the owner and in most policies also covers damage outside the home."], ["An extension of his AVP, so that damage to his own items is also covered.", "An AVP covers liability towards others, never damage to one's own items."], ["A continuous travel insurance, because he is on the road with the instrument.", "A travel insurance covers luggage during trips, not weekly use in the Netherlands."], ["A higher insured amount on his home contents policy for the saxophone.", "A higher insured amount changes nothing about the limited cover outside the home."]], "1d.7"],
["What information should an adviser in any case gather when advising on a personal insurance package?", [["Family situation, possessions, existing insurances and the customer's attitude to risk.", "Only with this picture can the adviser determine which risks the customer runs, what is already covered and which risks the customer can and wants to bear himself."], ["Only the maximum premium the customer wants to pay per month.", "The budget is relevant, but without knowledge of risks and existing covers no suitable advice can be given."], ["Only the insurances the customer wants to take out himself, because the initiative lies with the customer.", "The adviser must also identify risks that the customer does not mention himself; that is part of his duty of care."], ["The full pension overview and the tax return of the last five years.", "For non-life advice these documents are usually not needed; the inventory must fit the advice."]], "1c.1"],
["Why is it important for an adviser to record the advice and the considerations in a client file?", [["It enables him to demonstrate that he advised carefully and the advice can be followed.", "In the event of a complaint or dispute, the burden of proving careful service often lies in practice with the adviser. A good file shows what was discussed and why."], ["Because without a client file the insurer may not issue a policy.", "The insurer accepts on the basis of the application; the advice file is the adviser's responsibility."], ["Because the file replaces the policy conditions if they are unclear.", "The file can help in explaining the advice, but never replaces the policy conditions."], ["Because the client must sign the file before the premium can be collected.", "Premium collection does not depend on a signed advice file."]], "2e.17"],
["The Van Rijn family is having its home extensively renovated over the next four months: an extension is being built and the kitchen is being renewed. During the renovation the family is staying with relatives and the home stands empty at night. Building materials and tools are on the building site. Afterwards the rebuilding value is expected to be considerably higher. What is the best advice for the home insurance?", [["Report the renovation beforehand, so that cover during construction and the insured amount are correct.", "In most policies restrictions apply during renovation and vacancy; in addition, the insured amount must go up after the renovation to prevent underinsurance."], ["Report the new rebuilding value to the insurer only after the renovation.", "Risks already arise during the renovation, such as vacancy and theft of materials; the renovation must therefore be reported beforehand."], ["Do nothing, because annual indexation automatically absorbs the increase in value.", "Indexation follows the price development of building costs, not an extension of the home."], ["Temporarily suspend the buildings insurance during construction and have the contractor insure everything.", "The owner continues to run risk for damage to his home; the contractor's insurance does not fully take that over."]], "1c.9"],
["What does the Salvage Foundation (Stichting Salvage) do after a fire in a private home?", [["It provides immediate help on behalf of insurers, such as damage limitation and initial shelter.", "After a report from the fire brigade a salvage coordinator comes to the scene, takes damage-limiting measures and arranges initial shelter; the costs are borne by the insurers."], ["As an independent expert, it establishes which part of the fire damage is covered by the policy.", "Salvage does not assess cover; the insurer does that, possibly after the damage has been assessed by an expert."], ["It buys up the damaged goods and pays the proceeds to the insured.", "That is not Salvage's task; it focuses on immediate help and damage limitation."], ["It pays an advance on the compensation to the affected residents.", "Salvage is not a damage fund and does not pay compensation; the payment comes from the insurer."]], "1h.21"],
["A travel agency offers a cancellation insurance when a holiday is booked. How does the IDD classify the travel agency here?", [["As an ancillary insurance intermediary, because insurance is a supplement to the trip.", "The IDD has a lighter regime for parties that offer insurances as a supplement to a product or service. Core duties, such as a sale that fits the customer's needs, do apply."], ["As an insurer, because the travel agency itself bears the risk of cancellation.", "The travel agency does not bear the risk; the insurer for which the travel agency intermediates does."], ["As a managing agent (authorised agent), because the travel agency may itself issue policies.", "A managing agent has authority from the insurer to accept risks; a travel agency that offers insurance is therefore not an authorised agent."], ["It falls entirely outside the IDD, because travel insurances are not financial products.", "Travel and cancellation insurances are insurance products; the IDD also applies to this distribution."]], "1e.1"],
["Mrs Jansen tells her adviser that she is having a large extension built. The adviser does nothing with the notification. After a fire the home turns out to be underinsured. How is this assessed?", [["The adviser has probably breached his duty of care and may be liable for the customer's loss.", "A customer who reports a relevant change to her adviser may count on the adviser taking action, such as adjusting the insured amount. Failing to do so can be a professional error."], ["The adviser is not liable, because only the customer is responsible for reporting to the insurer.", "If the customer reports the change to her adviser, the adviser has a duty of care of his own to act on it."], ["The insurer must pay in full, because a report to the adviser always counts as a report to the insurer.", "Whether the adviser's knowledge is attributed to the insurer depends on the relationship; the question here concerns the adviser's duty of care."], ["There is no problem, because an extension always automatically falls under the existing home insurance.", "An extension raises the rebuilding value; without adjusting the insured amount, underinsurance in fact threatens."]], "1c.9"],
["Nadia (28) lives alone and is moving to her first rented apartment. When advised on her insurance package, she wants to keep the premium as low as possible. Against her adviser's advice, she decides not to take out an AVP. She thinks she is careful and that nothing is likely to happen. What must the adviser do?", [["Point out the risk of unlimited liability to her and record her decision.", "The customer ultimately decides for herself; the adviser must make the consequences clear and record the deviating decision in the file."], ["Take out the AVP anyway, because it is legally compulsory for every private individual.", "An AVP is not legally compulsory in the Netherlands; the customer may choose not to take one."], ["Stop advising, because she does not follow the adviser's advice.", "Not following advice is no reason to end the service; recording it is sufficient."], ["Record nothing, because the customer chose this herself.", "It is precisely a deviating choice that must be recorded, so that it is clear later that she was well informed."]], "1c.4"],
["Mrs Hermans (86) has dementia and can no longer live at home. She needs permanent supervision 24 hours a day and is moving to a nursing home. Her daughter thinks that her mother's basic insurance and supplementary insurance cover these costs. She asks the adviser how this works. What is the correct answer?", [["Residence in a nursing home falls under the Long-term Care Act (Wlz), after an assessment by the Care Needs Assessment Centre (CIZ) and with a personal contribution.", "The Wlz covers long-term care for people who permanently need care or supervision 24 hours a day; the CIZ gives the assessment and an income-related personal contribution applies."], ["The basic insurance reimburses the stay, because it concerns medically necessary care.", "The Health Insurance Act (Zvw) does not cover long-term residence in a nursing home; that falls under the Wlz."], ["The supplementary insurance reimburses the stay, if she has had it long enough.", "Supplementary insurances do not cover long-term nursing home care; that falls under the Wlz."], ["The municipality reimburses the stay in full under the Social Support Act (Wmo), without a personal contribution.", "The Wmo regulates support at home; residence with permanent 24-hour care falls under the Wlz, with a personal contribution."]], "1f.7"],
["What is an authorised agent (managing agent, volmachtbedrijf) in the non-life insurance market?", [["An intermediary who may accept risks on behalf of the insurer, issue policies and often handle claims.", "The insurer bears the risk, but leaves acceptance and administration to the authorised agent within agreed limits."], ["An intermediary who negotiates exclusively on behalf of the customer with insurers, without the insurer's powers.", "That describes an ordinary adviser; an authorised agent in fact has powers on behalf of the insurer."], ["A body designated by the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) to deal with complaints about insurers.", "Complaints are dealt with by Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute); an authorised agent is a market party."], ["A reinsurer that takes over part of an insurer's risk.", "A reinsurer takes over risk; an authorised agent does not itself bear any insurance risk."]], "1e.1"],
["What is the essence of insurers' code of conduct on informed renewal and contract terms for personal non-life insurances?", [["That the customer is informed in good time before a renewal, so that he can make a conscious choice.", "The code of conduct is meant to prevent a consumer from being unknowingly tied to an insurance; he is informed in good time before the renewal."], ["That insurers may never increase the premium on renewal.", "Premium adjustment on renewal is not prohibited; the point is that the customer is informed of it in good time."], ["That an insurance automatically ends after the first year if the customer does not respond.", "An insurance simply continues after renewal; the code focuses on timely information, not on automatic termination."], ["That a consumer may cancel an insurance only after three years.", "After tacit renewal a consumer can in fact cancel daily with a notice period of at most one month."]], "1a.11"],
["Saskia's car is six months old when she is hit by a motorist who is fully liable. The damage of €6,000 is professionally repaired and compensated by the WA insurer of the counterparty. Even so, her car is worth less on sale, because it has a claims history. She asks her adviser whether she can also claim something for that. What is the correct answer?", [["Yes, this loss in value can be claimed, using the Guideline on Depreciation of Passenger Cars (Richtlijn Waardevermindering Personenauto's).", "Even after repair, a car can be worth less; this guideline provides a method for determining that loss in value from a liable counterparty."], ["No, after professional repair there is no longer any damage that can still be compensated.", "Even after repair, a car can be worth less; that loss in value is damage that the liable party must compensate."], ["Only through her own casco insurance, with loss of no-claim years.", "The damage is recovered from the liable counterparty; her own casco insurance is not needed for that."], ["Only if she actually sells the car within a month after the repair.", "Loss in value does not depend on an immediate sale; it concerns the lower value due to the claims history."]], "1a.10"]
];
